     1	// Chief Command v2 — seat definitions (spec §4)
     2	//
     3	// Providers sit behind NAMED ROLES so any of them can be swapped without touching
     4	// orchestration logic. Nothing else in the harness may reference a provider by name —
     5	// only by seat id. Vendor policy drift is risk #1 on this project (§9).
     6	//
     7	// Hardware (Phase 0 RAM check, §2): M2 Pro, 16 GB. BELOW the spec's 36 GB threshold,
     8	// so the free grinder is a 7B coder, not the hoped-for 30B.
     9	//
    10	// ===========================================================================
    11	// THE ORGANIZING PRINCIPLE
    12	//
    13	// Every seat answers ONE of two questions: "how fast does it talk?" or "how well
    14	// does it think?" Nothing is good at both, because thinking well MEANS taking time.
    15	// So we don't fight the tension — we seat around it:
    16	//
    17	//   MOUTH   fast, dumb, never blocks. Dispatches and narrates.
    18	//   BRAIN   slow, deep. A dispatch target you are never waiting on.
    19	//   WORKHORSE  the best autonomous coder we can buy. Volume + quality.
    20	//   GRINDERS   cheap parallel attempts. Output NEVER ships unreviewed (DB-enforced).
    21	//   REVIEWER   escalation, highest judgment. Optional and droppable.
    22	// ===========================================================================
    23	
    24	// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    25	// MOUTH — the Talker. What Neill actually talks to.
    26	//
    27	// Speech-to-speech: audio in, audio out, ONE model, native tool calling. This is
    28	// what deletes the Whisper + Kokoro + Pipecat-plumbing stack that killed v1.
    29	//
    30	// DECISION DEFERRED ON PURPOSE. Both candidates are GA, sub-second, and speak the
    31	// SAME wire protocol (xAI is OpenAI-Realtime-compatible — you change the base URL).
    32	// So: build against the OpenAI Realtime event schema, keep base_url in config, and
    33	// the other becomes a ONE-LINE swap. Do not marry either one.
    34	//
    35	// DECIDED 2026-08-27 (owner), on current measured numbers, not a blog post:
    36	//   grok-voice-think-fast-2.0  $0.08/min ($4.80/hr)  0.70s to first sound  S2S 82.9
    37	//   grok-voice-think-fast-1.0  $0.05/min ($3.00/hr)  1.25s                 S2S 75.7
    38	//   gpt-realtime-2.1 (high)    ~$10.75/hr measured   1.21s                 S2S 79.1
    39	// Grok 2.0 is faster, better and less than half OpenAI's cost. Google is out (rule 5).
    40	// The voice FEELING dumb is the design (the mouth never answers; everything waits on
    41	// the 13s Chief) — the fix is the fast lane (#12), not a vendor swap. OpenAI stays a
    42	// one-line fallback (same wire protocol). Pinned by exact id; never a -latest alias.
    43	// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    44	[seats.mouth]
    45	// ⚠️ WHAT ACTUALLY RUNS (found 2026-08-27): the voice page and its token endpoint in
    46	// harness/server.py + harness/web/voice.html are hardwired to OpenAI's realtime API
    47	// over WebRTC. For weeks this block said "grok" while the code ignored it — a config
    48	// that lies. It now says what runs. The DECIDED target is Grok voice 2.0 (above);
    49	// getting there is a BUILD (xAI's realtime is WebSocket-only, so the browser audio
    50	// path must be rewritten, plus its own token step) — queued as the top voice task.
    51	provider = "openai-realtime"
    52	model = "gpt-realtime-2.1"                  // the FULL model: mini won't reliably call the tool
    53	base_url = "https://api.openai.com/v1/realtime"
    54	family = "gpt"
    55	tier = "metered"
    56	turn_detection = "semantic_vad"             // NOT push-to-talk. Owner override.
    57	eagerness = "low"                           // a driver pauses mid-sentence to merge
    58	interrupt_response = true                   // barge-in
    59	
    60	// VOICE HAS ITS OWN BUDGET, separate from the builders. OpenAI realtime measures
    61	// ~$10.75/hr with reasoning up (~$0.06-0.11/min with caching). 480c/day ≈ 45 min-1 hr.
    62	// The Grok 2.0 target is $4.80/hr for the same hour.
    63	daily_cap_cents = 480
    64	notes = "Fast + dumb BY DESIGN. Never blocks. Target: Grok voice 2.0 (build queued)."
    65	
    66	// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    67	// BRAIN — deep reasoning, planning, decomposition, judging the gauntlet.
    68	// SLOW BY DESIGN and that is fine: it is a dispatch target, never a conversation
    69	// partner. Its ~13s time-to-first-token is invisible because you are never waiting.
    70	//
    71	// Ultra mode is literally an orchestrator: it decomposes the task and spawns
    72	// parallel subagents trained to coordinate MID-task rather than merge at the end.
    73	// That is the reviewer-gauntlet shape (§6), native.
    74	//   Terminal-Bench 2.1: 88.8% single-agent / 91.9% Ultra — SOTA for agentic work.
    75	// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    76	// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    77	// MODEL TIERS — nothing gets the top model by default. Owner (2026-07-14):
    78	//     "If we do highest model every time for every build, on autonomous work, Claude
    79	//      and ChatGPT are gonna bottom out."
    80	//
    81	// THE KEY FACT: the binding constraint on autonomous work is RATE LIMITS, not money.
    82	// Claude and OpenAI are both flat-rate — you already paid. But you CANNOT BUY YOUR WAY
    83	// OUT OF A WEEKLY CAP. Every top-tier call spent on boilerplate is one you don't have
    84	// left for the thing that actually needed it.
    85	//
    86	// So the top tier is EARNED: you ask for it, the work is dangerous (auth/money/data),
    87	// it already failed twice, a reviewer flagged p0/p1, or it's a decision that's
    88	// expensive to reverse. Everything else runs light or standard. See tiering.py — the
    89	// escalation is a RULE, not a vibe, and every job records which tier it got AND WHY.
    90	// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    91	
    92	[seats.brain]
    93	provider = "codex"
    94	model = "gpt-5.6-sol"
    95	tier = "subscription"
    96	family = "gpt"
    97	
    98	// Owner: "Sol needs to be the brain, but prolly doesn't need to be ultra for most
    99	// convos. Maybe if it's managing a major spec and deciding builds and such, but
   100	// generally lower effort in convo."
   101	//
   102	// Measured, and he's right: Sol at FULL effort took over FIVE MINUTES on a real
   103	// question. In a conversation that isn't thorough — it's a hang. Claude at ~8s gave a
   104	// genuinely better answer. So Sol's heavy tier is reserved for what it's actually for:
   105	// specs, architecture, and pushback.
   106	model_light    = "gpt-5.6-luna"             // quick takes
   107	model_standard = "gpt-5.6-terra"            // most work
   108	model_heavy    = "gpt-5.6-sol"              // specs, decisions, pushback. EARNED.
   109	effort_light    = "low"
   110	effort_standard = "medium"
   111	effort_heavy    = "high"
   112	
   113	mode = "ultra"                              // ONLY at heavy. Coordinated subagents.
   114	max_threads = 6                             // spec said 8; the real default is 6
   115	max_depth = 1
   116	notes = "OAuth is TOLERATED, not sanctioned. Most likely seat to break. Keep API-key swap ready."
   117	
   118	// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   119	// WORKHORSE — volume autonomous coding. Migrations, refactors, test backfills.
   120	//
   121	// OWNER DECISION (2026-07-13): OpenAI, not Claude. Rationale below — and note the
   122	// reason is NOT the one in the spec.
   123	//
   124	// ⚠️ THE SPEC'S REASON IS WRONG. It says Claude headless draws from a metered credit
   125	// pool. It does NOT — Anthropic announced that, then PAUSED it before it took effect.
   126	// `claude -p` runs on normal subscription limits TODAY. Claude-as-workhorse would not
   127	// cost extra dollars.
   128	//
   129	// ✅ THE REAL REASON, which nobody had written down: RATE LIMITS, not cost. Running a
   130	// workhorse hard (unattended overnight builds, migrations, backfills) burns Max plan
   131	// usage caps — and it's the SAME bucket as interactive use. The failure mode is nasty:
   132	// your autonomous builder silently eats the model you were going to review its work
   133	// with. So Claude stays OUT of the high-volume seat, and stays IN as low-volume
   134	// escalation (see [seats.reviewer]).
   135	//
   136	// GPT-5.6-sol is not a consolation prize here: 88.8% Terminal-Bench 2.1 (91.9% Ultra),
   137	// SOTA for agentic coding. Flat subscription, predictable, bounded.
   138	//
   139	// For the record, since it may matter on the Studio later:
   140	//     Claude Opus  87.6% SWE-bench Verified   (best autonomous coder available)
   141	//     GPT-5.6-sol  64.6% SWE-bench Pro / 88.8% Terminal-Bench  (top-tier agentic)
   142	//     Grok Build   70.8% SWE-bench Verified   (weak model, great harness)
   143	// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   144	[seats.workhorse]
   145	provider = "codex"
   146	model = "gpt-5.6-sol"
   147	tier = "subscription"
   148	family = "gpt"
   149	worktree_isolated = true
   150	
   151	model_light    = "gpt-5.6-luna"             // scaffolding, boilerplate, first drafts
   152	model_standard = "gpt-5.6-terra"            // the bulk of real building
   153	model_heavy    = "gpt-5.6-sol"              // auth, money, data, or it failed twice
   154	notes = "OpenAI sub. Shares auth with [seats.brain] — see CONCENTRATION RISK below."
   155	
   156	// The Claude builders — Riggs (backend), Finn (frontend), Nova (glue). Same lanes,
   157	// same memory, tiered the same way. Opus is the best coder we own (87.6% SWE-bench);
   158	// it is also the one most likely to burn a weekly cap, so it is EARNED like the rest.
   159	[seats.riggs]
   160	provider = "claude-cli"
   161	model = "claude-opus-4-8"
   162	family = "claude"
   163	tier = "subscription"
   164	model_light    = "claude-haiku-4-5"
   165	model_standard = "claude-sonnet-5"
   166	model_heavy    = "claude-opus-4-8"          // the best coder we have. EARNED.
   167	notes = "Backend lane. Memory belongs to the LANE, not the model — swap the model, keep the scars."
   168	
   169	// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   170	// GROK — builder + cross-family reviewer.
   171	//
   172	// ⚠️ TWO EARLIER CLAIMS OF MINE WERE WRONG. Correcting both, on the record:
   173	//
   174	//   1. "Grok Build is weak (70.8% SWE-bench)." FALSE. That was grok-build-0.1, a
   175	//      legacy option nobody runs. Grok Build now DEFAULTS to grok-4.5. On a single
   176	//      consistent agentic-coding index: Grok 4.5 = 76, Codex = 76, Claude Code = 78.
   177	//      It is not the weak sibling. It matches Codex at ~half the cost/task ($2.49).
   178	//
   179	//   2. "The 403 is a Heavy-only tier gate." FALSE. It's an UNDISCLOSED MONTHLY QUOTA
   180	//      on standard SuperGrok. The 403 body lies — it says "you do not have an active
   181	//      Grok subscription" when you do and have merely run out. Users have been locked
   182	//      out after ~3 days of LIGHT cron use. Nobody publishes the quota.
   183	//
   184	// I also invented a workload ("throw 8 parallel attempts, let the gauntlet pick") to
   185	// compensate for a weakness that was my own measurement error. Dropped. Grok is just
   186	// a good, cheap builder. No exotic volume pattern needed.
   187	//
   188	// THE ANSWER TO THE HIDDEN QUOTA: don't choose between sub and API — run BOTH.
   189	// Subscription primary, metered API key as fallback on the same seat. When the
   190	// mystery quota dies at 2am mid-build, the seat fails over to the key and KEEPS
   191	// GOING instead of stalling the run. Subscription economics, metered reliability.
   192	//
   193	// HARD CEILING: $30 sub + $30/mo API cap = $60/mo maximum, no surprises.
   194	// (daily_cap_cents 100 x ~30 days = ~$30/mo of fallback.)
   195	// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   196	// GROK BUILDS LESS THAN THE OTHERS — deliberately. Owner (2026-07-13):
   197	//     "it can review as much as it wants more or less, but shouldn't build as much
   198	//      as claude and chat"
   199	//
   200	// He's right, and the economics agree. Claude and OpenAI are FLAT-RATE — a build on
   201	// those seats costs nothing marginal, it's already bought. Grok is METERED, so every
   202	// build is real money leaving the account. And builds are the EXPENSIVE kind of work
   203	// (token-heavy) while reviews are CHEAP (read-heavy, ~1/10 the tokens).
   204	//
   205	//     BUILD on the seats you already pay for.
   206	//     REVIEW on the metered one, because reviewing is cheap.
   207	//
   208	// Which lands Grok exactly where it's most valuable anyway. Its worth was never being
   209	// the strongest coder — it isn't. It's being a DIFFERENT MIND that catches what Claude
   210	// and GPT both miss. And that job costs pennies.
   211	[seats.grok]
   212	provider = "xai"
   213	model = "grok-4.5"                          // NOT grok-build-0.1 (legacy, weak)
   214	tier = "metered"                            // the runner calls the paid API with a key;
   215	family = "grok"                             // the SuperGrok sub covers the app, NOT this
   216	
   217	// Was OFF while the leaked xAI key was unrotated. Key rotated 2026-07-21; the runner
   218	// landed 2026-08-27 and the seat came back on the same day — a third mind on the panel.
   219	// If it must be turned off again, turn it OFF here rather than leaving it failing: an
   220	// enabled seat with no working key is a panel member that can never report. (A missing
   221	// key now excludes the seat at the door instead — see gauntlet.has_runner.)
   222	//
   223	// Owner decision, 2026-07-21: GROK DOES NOT TOUCH THE ARCH MEDICAL-RECORDS REPO. That
   224	// repo is out of scope entirely (Decision C); this line records the standing rule.
   225	disabled = false
   226	worktree_isolated = true
   227	daily_cap_cents  = 100                      // $1/day = ~$30/mo HARD CEILING
   228	build_cap_cents  = 25                       // ~25c/day of building — a RATION
   229	review_cap_cents = 75                       // reviewing is cheap; let it look at everything
   230	notes = "Reviews freely. Builds sparingly. Metered API, capped by the record."
   231	
   232	// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   233	// FREE GRINDER — the local junior. Overnight scaffolding, boilerplate, first drafts.
   234	// Sized for 16 GB. A junior dev, and treated as one: guard 1 in schema.sql makes it
   235	// STRUCTURALLY IMPOSSIBLE for its output to reach `done` unreviewed.
   236	// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   237	[seats.grinder_local]
   238	provider = "ollama"
   239	model = "qwen2.5-coder:7b"
   240	tier = "local"
   241	family = "qwen"
   242	endpoint = "http://127.0.0.1:11434"
   243	notes = "Bump to qwen3-coder:30b on the Studio (§8 phase 5). One line."
   244	
   245	// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   246	// REVIEWER — escalation only. Highest judgment: architecture calls, contested
   247	// verdicts, the bug the local model looped on three times.
   248	//
   249	// Claude stays in the hierarchy HERE, deliberately. At escalation volume (a handful
   250	// of calls a day) it will not dent the Max rate limits that disqualify it from the
   251	// workhorse seat — and it is already paid for.
   252	//
   253	// It earns its seat twice over: it is the strongest model on the board (87.6%
   254	// SWE-bench Verified), AND it is a FOURTH model family in the gauntlet. §6 exists
   255	// because different models miss different bugs. Claude catching what GPT and Grok
   256	// both missed is the entire point of keeping it.
   257	//
   258	// Per spec §4.4 this seat is optional and droppable — the system must run without it.
   259	// Never at the mouth: claude-cli spawns a subprocess per call (3-5s).
   260	// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   261	[seats.reviewer]
   262	provider = "claude-cli"                     // sanctioned path; subscription OAuth is NOT
   263	model = "claude-opus-4-8"
   264	tier = "subscription"                       // NOT credits — the pool was paused, never shipped
   265	family = "claude"
   266	optional = true
   267	max_calls_per_day = 25                      // keeps escalation from eating interactive headroom
   268	
   269	// Owner: "Reviewers prolly need to be opus and one model down for ChatGPT, unless we
   270	// say to go high review or we have a good trigger to do higher reviews."
   271	//
   272	// THE RULE: A REVIEWER IS NEVER WEAKER THAN THE BUILDER. A junior checking a senior's
   273	// work is theatre — it produces a green tick and no safety. So review tier FLOORS at
   274	// whatever tier built the thing. And reviews are cheap anyway (read-heavy, ~1/10th the
   275	// tokens of a build), so generosity here costs far less than generosity on the build.
   276	model_standard = "claude-sonnet-5"          // ordinary reviews
   277	model_heavy    = "claude-opus-4-8"          // dangerous areas, heavy builds, or you asked
   278	notes = "Low-volume escalation. Strongest model + 4th model family. Droppable by design."
   279	
   280	// ===========================================================================
   281	// ⚠️ CONCENTRATION RISK — read before changing anything above
   282	//
   283	// [seats.brain] and [seats.workhorse] BOTH run on the Codex OAuth path. That path is
   284	// TOLERATED, NOT SANCTIONED: no OpenAI doc blesses third-party OAuth, and their ToS
   285	// forbids sharing account credentials. Anthropic closed this exact door in Feb/Apr
   286	// 2026; Google closed it in Feb–June 2026 WITH ACCOUNT BANS. OpenAI is the last
   287	// vendor standing, and that is a TIMING position, not a policy position.
   288	//
   289	// If it closes, we lose the brain AND the builder in the same morning.
   290	//
   291	// Mitigation (cheap, and worth having ready rather than improvised under pressure):
   292	// both seats must swap to a paid API key by changing `provider`/`tier` here and
   293	// NOTHING else. That is what the seat abstraction is for. Test the swap before we
   294	// need it.
   295	// ===========================================================================
   296	
   297	// ===========================================================================
   298	// VOICE — how much it tells you, and when.
   299	//
   300	// THE RULE: one event stream, two renderings. The voice and the text are not two
   301	// systems — they read the SAME job records at different verbosity. Anything the
   302	// voice says, you could have read. Anything you can read, the voice can summarize.
   303	//
   304	//   VOICE  → a sentence.  "Riggs is on it."
   305	//   TEXT   → the terminal. Every tool call, every file, every result.
   306	//
   307	// Voice escalates on request. That's the interaction model: fast by default, deep
   308	// on demand — the same principle that put a quick model at the mouth and a slow one
   309	// at the brain.
   310	//
   311	// SHORT IS NOT A STYLE PREFERENCE — IT'S THE BILL. Audio output is 55-70% of the
   312	// realtime cost and runs ~16x the price of the same content as text. A chatty voice
   313	// is an expensive voice. §5.3 already asked for short spoken summaries with full
   314	// detail in the log; it was written for latency and it pays off twice.
   315	// ===========================================================================
   316	[voice]
   317	default_depth = "brief"                     // brief | normal | detailed
   318	
   319	// What each depth actually says:
   320	//   brief    "Riggs is on it."

 succeeded in 0ms:
     1	-- Chief Command v2 — harness job store
     2	--
     3	-- This is the layer OpenClaw does NOT give us. Its subagent sessions auto-archive
     4	-- after ~60 minutes and get soft-deleted; that is session archival, not durable job
     5	-- history. Spec §7 wants "what did the overnight run do?" to be answerable. This is that.
     6	--
     7	-- Two rules are enforced STRUCTURALLY here rather than in a prompt, because rules that
     8	-- live in prompts quietly stop holding at 4am:
     9	--   1. Local-model output can never reach `done` without a higher-tier passing review
    10	--      (spec §4.3 / §9 — "enforce in the pipeline itself, not by convention").
    11	--   2. Per-seat spend caps (OpenClaw core has no per-agent budget of any kind).
    12	
    13	PRAGMA journal_mode = WAL;
    14	PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON;
    15	
    16	-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    17	-- Seats: the named roles from spec §4. Providers are swappable behind these.
    18	-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    19	CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS seats (
    20	    id            TEXT PRIMARY KEY,          -- 'brain' | 'workhorse' | 'grinder_local' | 'reviewer' | 'tester' | 'mouth'
    21	    provider      TEXT NOT NULL,             -- 'codex' | 'xai' | 'ollama' | 'claude-cli' | ...
    22	    model         TEXT NOT NULL,
    23	    -- Model FAMILY, not provider. Two seats can share a family via different providers
    24	    -- (e.g. claude-cli and an Anthropic API key are both 'claude'). The gauntlet's
    25	    -- diversity rule and the no-self-testing guard both key off this, so it must be
    26	    -- the thing that actually determines shared blind spots.
    27	    family        TEXT NOT NULL,             -- 'gpt' | 'claude' | 'grok' | 'qwen'
    28	    -- tier drives the review guard below. 'local' output is never trusted on its own.
    29	    tier          TEXT NOT NULL CHECK (tier IN ('local', 'subscription', 'metered')),
    30	    -- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    31	    -- MODEL TIERS. Owner (2026-07-14):
    32	    --     "If we do highest model every time for every build, on autonomous work,
    33	    --      Claude and ChatGPT are gonna bottom out. I need us to build in model
    34	    --      tiering as well."
    35	    --
    36	    -- He's right, and it's the difference between a system that runs for a month and
    37	    -- one that dies on Thursday. Rate limits are the binding constraint on autonomous
    38	    -- work — NOT money (Claude and OpenAI are flat-rate). You cannot buy your way out
    39	    -- of a weekly cap. You can only spend it wisely.
    40	    --
    41	    -- So every seat carries THREE models, and the harness picks by what the work
    42	    -- actually deserves:
    43	    --
    44	    --   light     the cheap one. Boilerplate, scaffolding, small fixes, quick answers.
    45	    --   standard  the default. Most real work.
    46	    --   heavy     the top of the line. Reserved, and it has to be EARNED.
    47	    --
    48	    -- Nothing gets `heavy` by default. It's earned by: an explicit ask ("think hard"),
    49	    -- a risky area (auth, money, data), a repeat failure, or a major decision. See
    50	    -- tiering.py — the whole point is that the escalation is a rule, not a vibe.
    51	    -- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    52	    model_light    TEXT,     -- e.g. claude-haiku / gpt-5.6-luna
    53	    model_standard TEXT,     -- e.g. claude-sonnet / gpt-5.6-terra
    54	    model_heavy    TEXT,     -- e.g. claude-opus / gpt-5.6-sol  (EARNED, never default)
    55	    effort_light    TEXT DEFAULT 'low',
    56	    effort_standard TEXT DEFAULT 'medium',
    57	    effort_heavy    TEXT DEFAULT 'high',
    58	
    59	    -- CAPS ARE PER-ROLE, not just per-seat. Owner's call (2026-07-13):
    60	    --     "it can review as much as it wants more or less, but shouldn't build as
    61	    --      much as claude and chat"
    62	    --
    63	    -- The economics say the same thing. Claude and OpenAI are FLAT-RATE — a build on
    64	    -- those seats costs nothing marginal, it's already paid for. Grok is METERED, so
    65	    -- every build is real money. And builds are the expensive kind of work (token-heavy)
    66	    -- while reviews are cheap (read-heavy, ~1/10 the tokens).
    67	    --
    68	    -- So: BUILD on the seats you've already bought. REVIEW on the metered one, because
    69	    -- reviewing is cheap — and reviewing is what Grok is actually best at anyway. Its
    70	    -- value was never being the strongest coder. It's being a DIFFERENT MIND.
    71	    --
    72	    -- NULL = uncapped.
    73	    daily_cap_cents        INTEGER,   -- total across all roles (the hard ceiling)
    74	    build_cap_cents        INTEGER,   -- ration the expensive work
    75	    review_cap_cents       INTEGER,   -- reviewing is cheap; be generous
    76	    enabled       INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 1 CHECK (enabled IN (0, 1)),
    77	    notes         TEXT
    78	);
    79	
    80	-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    81	-- Projects: the top-level thing work belongs to. Each has its OWN memory.
    82	--
    83	-- Owner: "ability to see a projects tab and each project has its own memory,
    84	-- timeline/planning."
    85	--
    86	-- This matters more than it looks. Memory that isn't scoped to a project leaks:
    87	-- lessons from the EMR bleed into the harness, conventions from one codebase get
    88	-- applied to another. Chief already keeps per-AGENT memory; this is per-PROJECT,
    89	-- and the two are different axes. Riggs knows how HE works; the project knows how
    90	-- IT works.
    91	-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    92	CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS projects (
    93	    id            TEXT PRIMARY KEY,          -- 'chief', 'arch', 'jess'
    94	    name          TEXT NOT NULL,
    95	    repo_path     TEXT,
    96	    description   TEXT,
    97	    -- Where this project's own memory lives. Loaded into context for any job on it.
    98	    memory_dir    TEXT,
    99	    color         TEXT,                      -- the UI reads this
   100	    archived      INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0 CHECK (archived IN (0,1)),
   101	    created_at    TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT (datetime('now'))
   102	);
   103	
   104	-- The REAL projects, seeded so Chief knows them on ANY machine (a fresh DB or the Mac
   105	-- Studio), not just wherever a row happened to be typed. Without this, `projects` is
   106	-- empty on a new box and Chief goes back to improvising what Neill is working on — the
   107	-- exact bug this seed exists to kill. OR IGNORE so it never clobbers live edits.
   108	--
   109	-- repo_path: Arch is deliberately NULL. Chief may read Arch's NOTES (memory), but the
   110	-- fleet must never be pointed at its code or its patient data (Decision C, PHI). Dispatch
   111	-- does not route by repo_path today; if it ever learns to, THAT is where the guard goes.
   112	--
   113	-- memory_dir is NOT seeded here on purpose: it's the one genuinely machine-specific value
   114	-- (where this box keeps the project's notes), so it's set as local config per machine —
   115	-- keeping this file hardware-agnostic (rule 4).
   116	INSERT OR IGNORE INTO projects (id, name, repo_path, description, color) VALUES
   117	  ('chief', 'Chief Command', '~/code-projects/chief-command',
   118	   'The thing you talk to. This.', '#2E9BFF'),
   119	  ('jess', 'Jess', '~/code-projects/personal-assist',
   120	   'Personal assistant. Connects to Chief over the tailnet later.', '#A78BFA'),
   121	  ('arch', 'Arch (Arch to Freedom EMR)', NULL,
   122	   'The records system for the addiction-recovery facility — staff, beds, roles, approvals, the voice assistant Archie. Voice-first. Held at arm''s length here on purpose: Chief can read its notes but the fleet does not touch its code or its patient data.',
   123	   '#22c55e');
   124	
   125	-- Project memory: facts that belong to the PROJECT, not to an agent and not to a job.
   126	-- "The API returns snake_case." "Never touch the billing table directly."
   127	CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS project_memory (
   128	    id            INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,
   129	    project_id    TEXT NOT NULL REFERENCES projects(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
   130	    kind          TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'lesson'
   131	                  CHECK (kind IN ('lesson', 'convention', 'constraint', 'decision', 'gotcha')),
   132	    fact          TEXT NOT NULL,
   133	    -- Where it came from, so a wrong lesson can be traced and killed.
   134	    learned_from  INTEGER REFERENCES jobs(id) ON DELETE SET NULL,
   135	    created_at    TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT (datetime('now'))
   136	);
   137	
   138	CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_pmem_project ON project_memory(project_id);
   139	
   140	-- The plan. What's coming, in order. This is the timeline the UI draws.
   141	CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS plan_items (
   142	    id            INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,
   143	    project_id    TEXT NOT NULL REFERENCES projects(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
   144	    title         TEXT NOT NULL,
   145	    detail        TEXT,
   146	    phase         TEXT,                      -- 'phase 1', 'phase 2'…
   147	    status        TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'planned'
   148	                  CHECK (status IN ('planned', 'active', 'blocked', 'done', 'dropped')),
   149	    blocked_on    TEXT,                      -- plain English. "waiting on Neill to log in"
   150	    job_id        INTEGER REFERENCES jobs(id) ON DELETE SET NULL,
   151	    position      INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
   152	    created_at    TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT (datetime('now'))
   153	);
   154	
   155	CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_plan_project ON plan_items(project_id, position);
   156	
   157	-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   158	-- Todos: the checklist that belongs to a PROJECT, so it stops living in a
   159	-- terminal window and following nobody. Owner's annoyance, verbatim: "right now I
   160	-- have persistent todos that has to jump from window to window, which is annoying."
   161	--
   162	-- `owner_only` marks the items only Neill can do (rotate a key, flip a setting) —
   163	-- the ones the fleet must never quietly tick off on his behalf.
   164	-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   165	CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS todos (
   166	    id            INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,
   167	    project_id    TEXT NOT NULL REFERENCES projects(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
   168	    -- Sub-category WITHIN a project, so a project can hold several running lists
   169	    -- ("Security", "Build", "My tasks"). Owner: "ideally we can create a sub
   170	    -- category under each project as well." NULL / '' folds into a "General" list.
   171	    section       TEXT,
   172	    text          TEXT NOT NULL,
   173	    done          INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0 CHECK (done IN (0, 1)),
   174	    owner_only    INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0 CHECK (owner_only IN (0, 1)),
   175	    position      INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
   176	    created_at    TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT (datetime('now')),
   177	    done_at       TEXT
   178	);
   179	
   180	CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_todos_project ON todos(project_id, section, position, id);
   181	
   182	-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   183	-- Attachments: images and files Neill drops in — a screenshot of a bug, a mockup,
   184	-- a document — pinned to a project or a specific job. The bytes live on disk
   185	-- (gitignored); this row is how the UI finds them. captured_by is always the owner
   186	-- here; agent-produced evidence is the `artifacts` table, which has its own guards.
   187	-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   188	CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS attachments (
   189	    id            INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,
   190	    project_id    TEXT REFERENCES projects(id) ON DELETE SET NULL,
   191	    job_id        INTEGER REFERENCES jobs(id) ON DELETE SET NULL,
   192	    filename      TEXT NOT NULL,             -- the original name, for display
   193	    stored_path   TEXT NOT NULL,             -- where the bytes actually live
   194	    kind          TEXT NOT NULL CHECK (kind IN ('image', 'file')),
   195	    size_bytes    INTEGER,
   196	    created_at    TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT (datetime('now'))
   197	);
   198	
   199	CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_attachments_project ON attachments(project_id);
   200	CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_attachments_job ON attachments(job_id);
   201	
   202	-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   203	-- Jobs: one row per dispatched unit of work. This is the audit trail.
   204	-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   205	CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS jobs (
   206	    id            INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,
   207	    created_at    TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT (datetime('now')),
   208	    updated_at    TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT (datetime('now')),
   209	    started_at    TEXT,
   210	    finished_at   TEXT,
   211	
   212	    request       TEXT NOT NULL,             -- what was asked, verbatim (§7)
   213	    -- A 2-4 word handle the mouth coins at dispatch: "the rate limiter".
   214	    -- The VOICE says this. The TEXT shows `request` in full. Echoing a man's own
   215	    -- paragraph back at him is not how a colleague talks.
   216	    task_name     TEXT,
   217	    origin        TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'text'
   218	                  CHECK (origin IN ('text', 'voice', 'cron', 'relay')),  -- 'relay' = Jess (§10)
   219	
   220	    -- 'build'    → produces code. Ground truth = a screenshot (Playwright drove it).
   221	    -- 'research' → produces an ANSWER. Ground truth = a source (someone can go read it).
   222	    -- Same principle, different evidence. A researcher that can't cite is a researcher
   223	    -- that might be confabulating — and a confident wrong answer is worse than no answer,
   224	    -- because you act on it.
   225	    kind          TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'build' CHECK (kind IN ('build', 'research')),
   226	
   227	    project_id    TEXT REFERENCES projects(id) ON DELETE SET NULL,
   228	
   229	    -- Which tier this job actually ran at, and WHY. The 'why' matters: if we're
   230	    -- burning heavy tier on boilerplate, this column is how we find out.
   231	    tier          TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'standard'
   232	                  CHECK (tier IN ('light', 'standard', 'heavy')),
   233	    tier_reason   TEXT,
   234	
   235	    builder_seat  TEXT NOT NULL REFERENCES seats(id),
   236	    -- SNAPSHOTTED AT CREATION. Sol (cross-family review, 2026-07-13) found that
   237	    -- guards read the builder's tier/family LIVE from `seats`, so re-tiering a local
   238	    -- seat to 'subscription' retroactively legitimized its old unreviewed work.
   239	    -- Freeze it here. What a seat is TODAY cannot change what it WAS.
   240	    builder_tier   TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'local'
   241	                   CHECK (builder_tier IN ('local','subscription','metered')),
   242	    builder_family TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'unknown',
   243	    run_id        TEXT,                      -- OpenClaw sessions_spawn run id
   244	    session_key   TEXT,                      -- agent:<id>:subagent:<uuid>
   245	
   246	    -- Git as audit trail (§7). Worktree-per-job, main untouched until merged.
   247	    branch        TEXT,
   248	    worktree      TEXT,
   249	
   250	    -- THE VERSION UNDER REVIEW (Sol build gate 3, 2026-07-20). The exact content
   251	    -- hash the builder is putting forward — a git commit id. Reviews bind to this:
   252	    -- a verdict only counts toward completion while its reviewed_version matches.
   253	    -- The moment the builder changes the code, this changes, and every earlier
   254	    -- approval silently stops counting. Sol called the alternative the most
   255	    -- dangerous flaw in the system: "believable green checks on code nobody
   256	    -- reviewed." The gatekeeper's job at push time is to verify the code leaving
   257	    -- the building IS this hash — the DB proves the chain, git proves the content.
   258	    head_version  TEXT,
   259	
   260	    -- 'done'    = the gauntlet passed. The machine is satisfied.
   261	    -- 'shipped' = merged into the main line by the GATEKEEPER (Decision D, 2026-07-17:
   262	    --             the gauntlet IS the approval; Neill gates production DEPLOYS, not
   263	    --             merges). Guard 6 still requires a cross-family tester on the record.
   264	    -- (Earlier wording — "only Neill can move this" — predates Decision D.)
   265	    -- These are NOT the same thing, and conflating them is how agents launder
   266	    -- confidence. From Chief's memory, learned the hard way:
   267	    --     "Chief must NEVER say 'shipped' unless Neill has confirmed it works on
   268	    --      his device. 'Shipped' is reserved for Neill-confirmed-working."
   269	    -- That rule now lives here instead of in a prompt.
   270	    status        TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'todo'
   271	                  CHECK (status IN ('todo', 'in_progress', 'review', 'done',
   272	                                    'shipped', 'failed', 'cancelled')),
   273	    -- Set ONLY by the owner, through mark_shipped(). No agent has a path to this.
   274	    owner_confirmed_at TEXT,
   275	
   276	    -- How many passing reviews this job needs before it may complete. Set at
   277	    -- dispatch from the gauntlet config. Also from Chief's memory:
   278	    --     "Full set, always. No 'minimum,' no 'pick two,' no 'optional depending
   279	    --      on scope.'"
   280	    required_reviews INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
   281	
   282	    -- How many DISTINCT model families must have a passing review before done. This is
   283	    -- the floor "full set, always" doesn't itself guarantee: a full panel could still be
   284	    -- one family wearing three hats. Set at dispatch from the gauntlet's
   285	    -- min_model_families. Snapshotted and un-lowerable, like required_reviews. (task #10)
   286	    required_review_families INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
   287	
   288	    result        TEXT,                      -- final summary / diff ref
   289	    error         TEXT,
   290	
   291	    -- Voice wants a spoken-length answer; the text log keeps the detail (§5.3).
   292	    spoken_summary TEXT,
   293	
   294	    attempts      INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
   295	    parent_job_id INTEGER REFERENCES jobs(id) ON DELETE SET NULL,
   296	
   297	    -- DUPLICATE PROTECTION. A retry — a double-tap, a dropped connection, the voice
   298	    -- re-sending — must not start the same work twice. The caller stamps a key; a
   299	    -- second dispatch with the same key returns the existing job instead of spawning
   300	    -- a new one. NULL = no key given (nothing to dedupe against). The unique index
   301	    -- below lets many NULLs coexist but forbids two rows sharing a real key.
   302	    dispatch_key  TEXT
   303	);
   304	
   305	CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_jobs_status  ON jobs(status);
   306	CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_jobs_created ON jobs(created_at);
   307	CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_jobs_run     ON jobs(run_id);
   308	CREATE UNIQUE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_jobs_dispatch_key ON jobs(dispatch_key)
   309	    WHERE dispatch_key IS NOT NULL;
   310	
   311	-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   312	-- Verdicts: the gauntlet's output (§6). One row per reviewer per job.
   313	-- reviewer_tier is denormalized at write time so a later seat re-tiering cannot
   314	-- retroactively validate a job that was never properly reviewed.
   315	-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   316	CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS verdicts (
   317	    id            INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,
   318	    job_id        INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES jobs(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
   319	    reviewer_seat TEXT NOT NULL REFERENCES seats(id),
   320	    reviewer_tier TEXT NOT NULL CHECK (reviewer_tier IN ('local', 'subscription', 'metered')),
   321	    -- 'reviewer' reads the diff and judges. 'tester' DRIVES THE APP (Playwright) and
   322	    -- judges what it observed. The tester guards below apply only to the latter.
   323	    -- 'reviewer' reads the diff and judges it.
   324	    -- 'tester'   DROVE the running app (Playwright) and judges what it observed.
   325	    -- 'verifier' FACT-CHECKS a research answer against its sources.
   326	    role          TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'reviewer'
   327	                  CHECK (role IN ('reviewer', 'tester', 'verifier')),
   328	    -- model family is snapshotted here so §6's "at least two families" rule is
   329	    -- auditable, and so the no-self-family-testing guard has something to check.
   330	    model_family  TEXT NOT NULL,
   331	    -- WHAT, exactly, did this reviewer look at? Snapshotted at write time. A verdict
   332	    -- whose version no longer matches the job's head_version is history, not
   333	    -- approval — it stops counting the moment the code moves on. This is the other
   334	    -- half of the review-to-version chain.
   335	    reviewed_version TEXT,
   336	    verdict       TEXT NOT NULL CHECK (verdict IN ('pass', 'fail', 'needs_human')),
   337	    severity      TEXT CHECK (severity IN ('p0', 'p1', 'p2', 'p3')),
   338	    summary       TEXT,
   339	    detail        TEXT,
   340	    created_at    TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT (datetime('now'))
   341	);
   342	
   343	CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_verdicts_job ON verdicts(job_id);
   344	
   345	-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   346	-- CAPABILITIES — what an agent is PHYSICALLY ALLOWED to do.
   347	--
   348	-- This exists because Sol reviewed the architecture and demolished the previous
   349	-- safety story. The plan had been: the voice can act fast, and Chief watches and
   350	-- kills anything dumb. Sol's verdict:
   351	--
   352	--     "'Builders take minutes' measures how long they take to FINISH. What matters
   353	--      is how long they take to do their FIRST DAMAGING THING. That can be seconds.
   354	--      Chief and the builder would be RACING. There is no guarantee Chief wins."
   355	--
   356	--     "A fast supervisor is still only a fast witness if the builder can act first."
   357	--
   358	--     "A smarter model is not a security boundary."
   359	--
   360	-- He's right, and it's the difference between a bank with a vault and a bank with an
   361	-- attentive guard. You do not stop a robbery by watching carefully.
   362	--
   363	-- SO: agents get NO dangerous powers. Not "Chief will stop them" — THEY CANNOT.
   364	-- Reading code, writing code in their own worktree, running tests: always fine, and
   365	-- that's 95% of the work. Everything that touches the real world is denied by
   366	-- default and needs a signed, single-use permission slip for that exact act.
   367	-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   368	CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS capabilities (
   369	    seat_id       TEXT NOT NULL REFERENCES seats(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
   370	    capability    TEXT NOT NULL CHECK (capability IN (
   371	                      -- SAFE. Granted to every builder. This is the actual job.
   372	                      'read_code', 'write_worktree', 'run_tests', 'open_pr',
   373	                      -- DANGEROUS. Denied to everyone by default. Each needs a
   374	                      -- fresh, single-use approval naming the exact action.
   375	                      'touch_production', 'delete_data', 'run_migration',
   376	                      'deploy', 'merge_to_main', 'force_push',
   377	                      'read_secrets', 'send_external', 'spend_money'
   378	                  )),
   379	    PRIMARY KEY (seat_id, capability)
   380	);
   381	
   382	-- The safe set. Everything an agent needs to actually build software, and nothing
   383	-- that can hurt you. Note what ISN'T here — that's the whole point.
   384	CREATE VIEW IF NOT EXISTS safe_capabilities AS
   385	SELECT 'read_code' AS capability UNION ALL SELECT 'write_worktree'
   386	UNION ALL SELECT 'run_tests' UNION ALL SELECT 'open_pr';
   387	
   388	-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   389	-- APPROVALS — a signed, single-use permission slip for ONE exact dangerous act.
   390	--
   391	-- Sol on why a "yes" cannot be trusted on its own:
   392	--
   393	--     Chief: "First make a backup, then remove the old accounts."
   394	--     Neill: "Yes — skip the first one."
   395	--
   396	--     "That LOOKS like a confirmation, but it changes the safe plan into a dangerous
   397	--      one. Sending it directly recreates the original classification problem."
   398	--
   399	-- So an approval is not a word. It is a ROW: a numbered, one-time permission for a
   400	-- specific action, with the consequence written out in plain English and READ BACK to
   401	-- Neill before he can agree. It expires the moment the plan changes.
   402	--
   403	--     "A bare 'yes' should never approve something that was not just read back
   404	--      exactly." — which also handles the fact that a car is a terrible place to be
   405	--      understood correctly.
   406	-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   407	CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS approvals (
   408	    id            INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,
   409	    job_id        INTEGER REFERENCES jobs(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
   410	    capability    TEXT NOT NULL,            -- the ONE dangerous thing this permits
   411	    -- Exactly what will happen. Not a category — the actual act.
   412	    action        TEXT NOT NULL,            -- "delete 4,200 accounts inactive since 2024-01-01"
   413	    -- What Chief SAID OUT LOUD to Neill before he agreed. If this is empty, nothing
   414	    -- was read back, and a 'yes' means nothing.
   415	    read_back     TEXT NOT NULL,
   416	    -- Is this undoable, and how? Sol: "Anything irreversible needs a tested recovery
   417	    -- method BEFORE it starts."
   418	    reversible    INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0 CHECK (reversible IN (0,1)),
   419	    recovery      TEXT,                     -- "restore from the snapshot taken at 09:14"
   420	
   421	    granted_by    TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'owner' CHECK (granted_by = 'owner'),
   422	    granted_at    TEXT,
   423	    expires_at    TEXT NOT NULL,            -- short. Minutes, not hours.
   424	    used_at       TEXT,                     -- single use. Once spent, it's spent.
   425	    revoked_at    TEXT,
   426	    created_at    TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT (datetime('now'))
   427	);
   428	
   429	CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_approvals_job ON approvals(job_id);
   430	
   431	-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   432	-- GUARD 12 — a dangerous act needs a live, unused, read-back approval.
   433	-- No approval, no action. Not "Chief said it was fine" — a signed slip, or nothing.
   434	-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   435	CREATE VIEW IF NOT EXISTS live_approvals AS
   436	SELECT * FROM approvals
   437	 WHERE granted_at IS NOT NULL
   438	   AND used_at    IS NULL
   439	   AND revoked_at IS NULL
   440	   AND expires_at > datetime('now')
   441	   AND TRIM(read_back) <> '';
   442	
   443	-- An approval may never be BORN granted. (Sol, round 3 — verified 2026-07-14.)
   444	--
   445	-- Every approval guard fired on UPDATE OF granted_at only — the exact "born done" hole
   446	-- Sol found on the jobs table in round 1, never applied here. Confirmed with a live
   447	-- exploit: an INSERT with granted_at already set, reversible=0 and no recovery plan sailed
   448	-- straight into the live_approvals view. An approval must be born ungranted and become
   449	-- granted through an UPDATE, where the read-back and recovery guards below actually fire.
   450	CREATE TRIGGER IF NOT EXISTS guard_no_approval_is_born_granted
   451	BEFORE INSERT ON approvals
   452	WHEN NEW.granted_at IS NOT NULL
   453	BEGIN
   454	    SELECT RAISE(ABORT, 'guard: an approval cannot be created already granted — it must be granted through a read-back');
   455	END;
   456	
   457	-- An approval cannot be granted without something having been read back.
   458	CREATE TRIGGER IF NOT EXISTS guard_no_approval_without_readback
   459	BEFORE UPDATE OF granted_at ON approvals
   460	WHEN NEW.granted_at IS NOT NULL AND TRIM(COALESCE(NEW.read_back, '')) = ''
   461	BEGIN
   462	    SELECT RAISE(ABORT, 'guard: nothing was read back to him — a yes to nothing is not a yes');
   463	END;
   464	
   465	-- An irreversible act cannot be approved without a recovery plan.
   466	-- Sol: "'Kill' must never be presented as 'undo.'"
   467	CREATE TRIGGER IF NOT EXISTS guard_irreversible_needs_a_way_back
   468	BEFORE UPDATE OF granted_at ON approvals
   469	WHEN NEW.granted_at IS NOT NULL AND NEW.reversible = 0
   470	     AND TRIM(COALESCE(NEW.recovery, '')) = ''
   471	BEGIN
   472	    SELECT RAISE(ABORT, 'guard: this cannot be undone and has no way back — not without a recovery plan');
   473	END;
   474	
   475	-- Single use. A permission slip is spent when it is used.
   476	CREATE TRIGGER IF NOT EXISTS guard_an_approval_is_used_once
   477	BEFORE UPDATE OF used_at ON approvals
   478	WHEN OLD.used_at IS NOT NULL
   479	BEGIN
   480	    SELECT RAISE(ABORT, 'guard: that permission was already spent');
   481	END;
   482	
   483	-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   484	-- Events: the live activity stream. What an agent is doing RIGHT NOW.
   485	--
   486	-- This is what makes the text channel feel like the Claude Code terminal — you
   487	-- watch Riggs read auth.py, edit routes.py, run pytest, and come back green.
   488	-- Without it the harness can only tell you a job "happened," which is useless
   489	-- while you're waiting and only mildly interesting afterward.
   490	--
   491	-- ONE STREAM, TWO RENDERINGS (this is the key design decision):
   492	--   VOICE reads jobs.spoken_summary — a sentence. "Riggs is on it, two minutes."
   493	--   TEXT  reads THIS table in full   — every tool call, every file, every result.
   494	-- Same truth, different verbosity. The voice can escalate into this table on
   495	-- request ("what's it actually doing?") — see [voice] in seats.toml.
   496	--
   497	-- Note `lane` and `model` are BOTH recorded. The lane (Riggs) carries the memory
   498	-- and the conventions; the model is whoever is sitting in that chair today. You
   499	-- must always be able to see which — otherwise you can't tell whether your auth
   500	-- module was built by your best coder or your cheapest one.
   501	-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   502	CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS events (
   503	    id            INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,
   504	    job_id        INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES jobs(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
   505	    seat_id       TEXT NOT NULL REFERENCES seats(id),
   506	    lane          TEXT NOT NULL,             -- 'riggs' | 'finn' | 'nova' | 'forge' ...
   507	    model         TEXT NOT NULL,             -- who is actually in the chair right now
   508	    family        TEXT NOT NULL,
   509	    -- 'skipped' is load-bearing (task #10): the record needs a word for something that
   510	    -- did NOT happen. A reviewer that was capped out or couldn't run must leave a mark,
   511	    -- or a panel that quietly shrank looks identical to a full one. (Migration 006.)
   512	    kind          TEXT NOT NULL CHECK (kind IN (
   513	                      'dispatched', 'thinking', 'read', 'edit', 'write',
   514	                      'command', 'test_run', 'browse', 'verdict', 'done', 'error',
   515	                      'skipped'
   516	                  )),
   517	    target        TEXT,                      -- the file / command / URL it touched
   518	    detail        TEXT,                      -- one line, terminal-style
   519	    created_at    TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT (datetime('now'))
   520	);

