docs/sol/sol_out_v2.txt:4074:Then build an attack test for every agent type and every OpenClaw upgrade. The test should deliberately try to bypass the job record, forge evidence, change the protected version, spend beyond the limit, reach production, alter the rulebook, and reuse an approval. If any runner can do one of those things, that runner remains disabled until the route is closed.
docs/sol/sol_out_v2.txt:4157:Then build an attack test for every agent type and every OpenClaw upgrade. The test should deliberately try to bypass the job record, forge evidence, change the protected version, spend beyond the limit, reach production, alter the rulebook, and reuse an approval. If any runner can do one of those things, that runner remains disabled until the route is closed.
docs/sol/sol_round3.out:575:  deliberately, each upgrade re-running the attack test before being trusted. **Security
docs/sol/sol_round3.out:670:14:You have reviewed this system four times and found serious weaknesses each time — nine flaws in the database rules, then eighteen more, then the finding that the "capability gate" was ineffective because it governed the record rather than the action, and in round 4 the deepest finding yet: an agent can put a harmful payload into its working copy, fool the model reviewers, receive genuine passing records, and have the release service deliberately deploy it — no forgery required, every door working exactly as designed. You rejected the design and listed 12 required changes, three of which were owner decisions.
docs/sol/sol_round3.out:807:| 9. Full containment profile | **Addressed only in words** | Every runner, child process, tool server, hook, schedule, retry, and resource limit is named ([containment profile](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:108)). But no actual profile, limits, network layout, or enforcement owner is specified. “Specified and tested” is a promise to write the missing design later. |
