docs/sol/sol_round1.out:7221:No database forgery or direct release-service call is required. Every stated door can work exactly as designed and the harmful action still occurs.
docs/sol/sol_round1.out:7334:The design does not specify a protected identity for each reviewer response, how responses are bound to one run, or how a forged or replayed completion is rejected. More importantly, a genuine reviewer can be fooled. “The harness received this response” is not the same as “the reviewed code is safe.”
docs/sol/sol_round1.out:7608:No database forgery or direct release-service call is required. Every stated door can work exactly as designed and the harmful action still occurs.
docs/sol/sol_round1.out:7721:The design does not specify a protected identity for each reviewer response, how responses are bound to one run, or how a forged or replayed completion is rejected. More importantly, a genuine reviewer can be fooled. “The harness received this response” is not the same as “the reviewed code is safe.”
docs/sol/sol_out.txt:41:A table of what each agent may do. Safe: read code, write in own worktree, run tests, open a PR. Denied by default: touch production, delete data, deploy, merge to main, force push, read secrets, send external requests, spend money. Anything irreversible needs a single-use approval naming the exact action, with what was READ BACK ALOUD to Neill, and a recovery plan.
docs/sol/sol_out.txt:339:- Gauntlet flow: builder produces work → up to 6 reviewers run **in parallel** as Codex subagents (mini-tier models) → tester agent validates → disagreements or flagged items escalate to the Claude seat or to Neill's interactive review queue.
