docs/dispatch-bridge-glue-spec.md-12----
docs/dispatch-bridge-glue-spec.md-13-
docs/dispatch-bridge-glue-spec.md-14-## What the hook does
docs/dispatch-bridge-glue-spec.md-15-
docs/dispatch-bridge-glue-spec.md:16:Before every user turn hits `_handle_text_turn` (Riggs's memory-loaded LLM
docs/dispatch-bridge-glue-spec.md-17-path), the hook:
docs/dispatch-bridge-glue-spec.md-18-
docs/dispatch-bridge-glue-spec.md-19-1. Runs `detect_project_switch` (Riggs's regex) — scope changes don't need
docs/dispatch-bridge-glue-spec.md-20-   a classifier call.
docs/dispatch-bridge-glue-spec.md-21-2. Runs `classify_intent` (Nova's classifier) → returns one of
docs/dispatch-bridge-glue-spec.md-22-   `chat | task | status | cancel`.
docs/dispatch-bridge-glue-spec.md-23-3. Routes:
docs/dispatch-bridge-glue-spec.md:24:   - `chat` → Riggs's `_handle_text_turn` (memory-equipped API response)
docs/dispatch-bridge-glue-spec.md-25-   - `task` → `dispatcher.dispatch(...)` with narration
docs/dispatch-bridge-glue-spec.md-26-   - `status` → summarize running task stdout via Haiku + TTS
docs/dispatch-bridge-glue-spec.md-27-   - `cancel` → `dispatcher.cancel(...)`
docs/dispatch-bridge-glue-spec.md-28-
--
docs/dispatch-bridge-glue-spec.md-307-## Sanity tests (after glue lands)
docs/dispatch-bridge-glue-spec.md-308-
docs/dispatch-bridge-glue-spec.md-309-Forge should verify:
docs/dispatch-bridge-glue-spec.md-310-
docs/dispatch-bridge-glue-spec.md:311:1. `"What's Chief Command?"` → chat → memory-equipped LLM reply (Riggs's path).
docs/dispatch-bridge-glue-spec.md-312-2. `"Build a smoke test for the login page."` → task → subprocess spawns in
docs/dispatch-bridge-glue-spec.md-313-   `~/Desktop/chief-command`, stdout streams, voice narrates dispatch.
docs/dispatch-bridge-glue-spec.md-314-3. Mid-dispatch: `"status?"` → Haiku summarizes current stdout, voice reads it.
docs/dispatch-bridge-glue-spec.md-315-4. Mid-dispatch: `"stop"` → subprocess killed via SIGTERM → SIGKILL after 5s.
--
docs/CC_REBOOT_SPEC_2026-07-11.md-20-- **Networking:** Tailscale mesh. The harness binds to the tailnet, never the public internet. Phone and MacBook reach it via Tailscale hostname. No port forwarding, no exposed services.
docs/CC_REBOOT_SPEC_2026-07-11.md-21-
docs/CC_REBOOT_SPEC_2026-07-11.md-22-## 3. Core harness: OpenClaw
docs/CC_REBOOT_SPEC_2026-07-11.md-23-
docs/CC_REBOOT_SPEC_2026-07-11.md:24:OpenClaw is the orchestration backbone — gateway, session management, agent memory, and the routing layer to all model providers.
docs/CC_REBOOT_SPEC_2026-07-11.md-25-
docs/CC_REBOOT_SPEC_2026-07-11.md-26-Requirements:
docs/CC_REBOOT_SPEC_2026-07-11.md-27-- Install pinned to a specific version. **No auto-updates.** Version bumps are deliberate, tested events (the Gemini CLI shutdown is the cautionary tale — vendor and tooling behavior changes under you).
docs/CC_REBOOT_SPEC_2026-07-11.md-28-- All provider connections defined in config, each swappable independently.
--
docs/CC_REBOOT_SPEC_2026-07-11.md-54-
docs/CC_REBOOT_SPEC_2026-07-11.md-55-### 4.4 Escalation reviewer — Claude (Max subscription + credit pool)
docs/CC_REBOOT_SPEC_2026-07-11.md-56-- Two modes, and the distinction matters:
docs/CC_REBOOT_SPEC_2026-07-11.md-57-  - **Interactive (subscription, effectively free):** Neill opens a Claude Code session (locally or via SSH) and reviews the gauntlet's flagged queue himself with Claude. Human-initiated = subscription limits.
docs/CC_REBOOT_SPEC_2026-07-11.md:58:  - **Headless (credit pool):** the harness may invoke `claude -p` for automated deep reviews, drawing on the Max plan's included monthly agent credits. Budget-cap this in config (e.g., N automated Claude reviews/day) so it never silently overruns the free allotment.
docs/CC_REBOOT_SPEC_2026-07-11.md-59-- Role: highest-judgment work — architecture review, contested reviewer verdicts, the bug the local model looped on three times.
docs/CC_REBOOT_SPEC_2026-07-11.md-60-- This seat is optional/droppable. The system must run fully without it.
docs/CC_REBOOT_SPEC_2026-07-11.md-61-
docs/CC_REBOOT_SPEC_2026-07-11.md-62-### 4.5 Explicitly excluded
--
docs/CC_REBOOT_SPEC_2026-07-11.md-64-
docs/CC_REBOOT_SPEC_2026-07-11.md-65-## 5. Voice layer (the product)
docs/CC_REBOOT_SPEC_2026-07-11.md-66-
docs/CC_REBOOT_SPEC_2026-07-11.md-67-### 5.1 The core architecture decision — voice skin ≠ brain
docs/CC_REBOOT_SPEC_2026-07-11.md:68:The voice layer and the orchestrator are **separate**. A fast, local voice front-end handles listening and speaking; it makes **tool calls** to the GPT-5.6 orchestrator (Codex, §4.1) which holds the harness memory, subagents, and reasoning. You are talking *to your actual orchestrator* — the local pipeline is just its ears and mouth. This is what kills the Chief Command latency wound: the voice layer acknowledges in ~300ms ("on it, kicking that off") while 5.6 reasons and builds underneath. Perceived latency is the voice layer's, not the builder's.
docs/CC_REBOOT_SPEC_2026-07-11.md-69-
docs/CC_REBOOT_SPEC_2026-07-11.md-70-This means you get 5.6's full intelligence — interrogating a build, pushing back, inspecting its own work — with local, free, low-latency voice on top. The orchestrator is never a dumbed-down voice model; it's the real brain with all the context and subagents.
docs/CC_REBOOT_SPEC_2026-07-11.md-71-
docs/CC_REBOOT_SPEC_2026-07-11.md-72-### 5.2 Interaction model
