docs/sol/sol_round5.out-485-docs/CC_REBOOT_SPEC_2026-07-11.md:28:- All provider connections defined in config, each swappable independently.
docs/sol/sol_round5.out-486-docs/CC_REBOOT_SPEC_2026-07-11.md:31:- Text chat interface is the first deliverable and permanent fallback. If voice dies, text always works.
docs/sol/sol_round5.out-487-docs/CC_REBOOT_SPEC_2026-07-11.md:35:Four seats. Each one behind a named role in config so providers can be swapped without touching orchestration logic.
docs/sol/sol_round5.out-488-docs/CC_REBOOT_SPEC_2026-07-11.md:52:  - Studio phase (64GB): Qwen3-Coder 30B resident full-time alongside the voice stack.
docs/sol/sol_round5.out:489:docs/CC_REBOOT_SPEC_2026-07-11.md:58:  - **Headless (credit pool):** the harness may invoke `claude -p` for automated deep reviews, drawing on the Max plan's included monthly agent credits. Budget-cap this in config (e.g., N automated Claude reviews/day) so it never silently overruns the free allotment.
docs/sol/sol_round5.out-490-docs/CC_REBOOT_SPEC_2026-07-11.md:65:## 5. Voice layer (the product)
docs/sol/sol_round5.out-491-docs/CC_REBOOT_SPEC_2026-07-11.md:67:### 5.1 The core architecture decision — voice skin ≠ brain
docs/sol/sol_round5.out:492:docs/CC_REBOOT_SPEC_2026-07-11.md:68:The voice layer and the orchestrator are **separate**. A fast, local voice front-end handles listening and speaking; it makes **tool calls** to the GPT-5.6 orchestrator (Codex, §4.1) which holds the harness memory, subagents, and reasoning. You are talking *to your actual orchestrator* — the local pipeline is just its ears and mouth. This is what kills the Chief Command latency wound: the voice layer acknowledges in ~300ms ("on it, kicking that off") while 5.6 reasons and builds underneath. Perceived latency is the voice layer's, not the builder's.
docs/sol/sol_round5.out-493-docs/CC_REBOOT_SPEC_2026-07-11.md:70:This means you get 5.6's full intelligence — interrogating a build, pushing back, inspecting its own work — with local, free, low-latency voice on top. The orchestrator is never a dumbed-down voice model; it's the real brain with all the context and subagents.
docs/sol/sol_round5.out-494-docs/CC_REBOOT_SPEC_2026-07-11.md:77:Decision: **local Whisper + Kokoro, NOT OpenAI realtime voice.** Rationale is cost at Neill's usage pattern (a couple hours/day, indefinitely):
docs/sol/sol_round5.out-495-docs/CC_REBOOT_SPEC_2026-07-11.md:78:- OpenAI's realtime voice (gpt-realtime-2) does **not** run on the ChatGPT/Codex subscription — it's separate metered OpenAI Platform API billing (~$0.18–0.46/min for the full model). At 2 hrs/day that's roughly **$700–1000+/month**. Even gpt-realtime-mini with prompt caching (~$0.05–0.10/min) lands around **$150–250/month** for daily hours-long use. Not worth it for a single-user personal builder.
docs/sol/sol_round5.out-496-docs/CC_REBOOT_SPEC_2026-07-11.md:85:- Voice responses should be *spoken-length* — the harness prompts models for a short verbal summary, with full detail written to the session log/text UI.
--
docs/sol/sol_round5.out-507-docs/OSS_RESEARCH_2026-07-11.md:22:| 3 | Codex OAuth for third-party harnesses is **"sanctioned by OpenAI"** (§4.1) | **WRONG as stated.** It *works* — it's what OpenClaw's OpenAI provider uses — but no OpenAI doc sanctions it, and OpenAI's ToS forbids sharing account credentials / programmatic extraction outside permitted surfaces. It is **tolerated, not sanctioned**. | **This is the biggest latent risk in the spec.** See §5 below. |
docs/sol/sol_round5.out-508-docs/OSS_RESEARCH_2026-07-11.md:23:| 4 | OpenAI realtime voice ~$0.18–0.46/min → $700–1000/mo (§5.3) | **WRONG — off by ~10×.** See **§1c**, which supersedes this row. Real figure at Neill's usage: **~$25/mo (mini), ~$76/mo (full).** The architectural half (not in the subscription; separate metered billing) is CONFIRMED. Model name stale (`-2.1`). | **The sole stated justification for the local voice stack has collapsed.** Re-litigate on true numbers. Also kill the "realtime = GPT-5.6" premise — it's a distilled family with a Sept-2024 cutoff. |
docs/sol/sol_round5.out-509-docs/OSS_RESEARCH_2026-07-11.md:26:| 7 | Voice STT: faster-whisper or whisper.cpp (§5.3) | **WRONG for this hardware.** faster-whisper has **no Metal support** on Apple Silicon — it silently falls back to CPU. | Use **MLX Whisper**. It's 30–40% faster than whisper.cpp *and* ships in Pipecat as `WhisperSTTServiceMLX`. Faster and less work — not a tradeoff. |
docs/sol/sol_round5.out-510-docs/OSS_RESEARCH_2026-07-11.md:33:subscription model at the head that can do two-way voice and isn't slow af."*
docs/sol/sol_round5.out:511:docs/OSS_RESEARCH_2026-07-11.md:57:§5.1 has the voice layer tool-calling "the GPT-5.6 orchestrator which holds the harness memory,
docs/sol/sol_round5.out-512-docs/OSS_RESEARCH_2026-07-11.md:58:subagents, and reasoning." **That routes every voice turn through a deep reasoning model** — even
docs/sol/sol_round5.out-513-docs/OSS_RESEARCH_2026-07-11.md:65:Kahneman's System 1 / System 2. The voice community converged on the same shape independently
docs/sol/sol_round5.out-514-docs/OSS_RESEARCH_2026-07-11.md:69:  smart.** It is ears, mouth, and dispatcher. Sub-second TTFT or the voice loop feels dead.
docs/sol/sol_round5.out-515-docs/OSS_RESEARCH_2026-07-11.md:85:**CLI-based runtimes cannot be in a per-turn voice loop:**
--
