docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6151-## 1. Spec corrections (do these first)
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6152-
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6153-| # | Spec claim | Verdict | Action |
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6154-|---|---|---|---|
docs/sol/sol_round4.out:6155:| 1 | Claude headless `claude -p` draws from a Max-plan **monthly agent-credit pool** (§4.4) | **WRONG** — announced, then **paused before taking effect**. Anthropic's own support article: *"We're pausing the changes… For now, nothing has changed."* `claude -p` draws on normal subscription limits. | Delete §4.4's credit-pool mechanics and the budget-cap-as-necessity framing. Keep headless usage *isolated* as forward-proofing (Anthropic says it will re-propose with notice). **Do keep** the real constraint: subscription OAuth tokens in third-party tools are prohibited (terms Feb 20 2026, billing enforcement Apr 4 2026). |
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6156-| 2 | Codex subagents: **up to 8 parallel workers** (§4.1) | **WRONG** — `agents.max_threads` **defaults to 6**. The "8" looks like contamination from the Grok Build claim. (`max_depth = 1` and the `~/.codex/agents/` TOML layout are **correct**; GA March 2026 **confirmed**.) | Fix fan-out arithmetic to 6, or set `max_threads` explicitly and validate empirically. |
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6157-| 3 | Codex OAuth for third-party harnesses is **"sanctioned by OpenAI"** (§4.1) | **WRONG as stated.** It *works* — it's what OpenClaw's OpenAI provider uses — but no OpenAI doc sanctions it, and OpenAI's ToS forbids sharing account credentials / programmatic extraction outside permitted surfaces. It is **tolerated, not sanctioned**. | **This is the biggest latent risk in the spec.** See §5 below. |
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6158-| 4 | OpenAI realtime voice ~$0.18–0.46/min → $700–1000/mo (§5.3) | **WRONG — off by ~10×.** See **§1c**, which supersedes this row. Real figure at Neill's usage: **~$25/mo (mini), ~$76/mo (full).** The architectural half (not in the subscription; separate metered billing) is CONFIRMED. Model name stale (`-2.1`). | **The sole stated justification for the local voice stack has collapsed.** Re-litigate on true numbers. Also kill the "realtime = GPT-5.6" premise — it's a distilled family with a Sept-2024 cutoff. |
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6159-| 5 | Google/Gemini excluded — ToS bans third-party OAuth (names OpenClaw), consumer CLI dead June 18 2026 (§4.5) | **CONFIRMED, both halves.** ToS names OpenClaw explicitly; mass bans were *enforced* Feb 2026; consumer access ended June 18. | No change. §4.5 is correct. |
--
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6188-question was never "which vendor" — it's "how hard is the thing you're talking to trying to think."
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6189-
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6190-### The design flaw
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6191-
docs/sol/sol_round4.out:6192:§5.1 has the voice layer tool-calling "the GPT-5.6 orchestrator which holds the harness memory,
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6193-subagents, and reasoning." **That routes every voice turn through a deep reasoning model** — even
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6194-"what did the overnight run do?" The spec then papers over it with a canned ~300ms "on it, kicking
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6195-that off" while the slow model grinds. That's a bandaid on a design problem.
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6196-
--
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6218-### ⚠️ The landmine — this kills the Claude Max head, and possibly the whole subscription premise
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6219-
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6220-**CLI-based runtimes cannot be in a per-turn voice loop:**
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6221-
docs/sol/sol_round4.out:6222:- `claude -p` subprocess: **3–5s overhead per call.** Cited discussion says this makes "use cases
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6223-  like voice assistants **prohibitively slow**."
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6224-- Claude Agent SDK `query()`: **~12s overhead per call**, no hot-process reuse
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6225-  ([claude-agent-sdk-typescript#34](https://github.com/anthropics/claude-agent-sdk-typescript/issues/34)).
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6226-- With process pre-warming/reuse: drops to <1s. Without it, you eat the full cost **every turn**.
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6227-- Direct Messages API: 1–3s end-to-end.
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6228-
docs/sol/sol_round4.out:6229:This collides head-on with the constraint from §1: **`agentRuntime.id: "claude-cli"` is the only
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6230-*legal* way to use the Claude Max subscription (Anthropic bans subscription OAuth in third-party
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6231-tools) — and it is exactly the path that costs 3–5s per turn.**
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6232-
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6233-> **You cannot have both "Claude Max subscription" and "snappy voice head." They are mutually
--
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6251-  agentic tool-calling, no CoT overhead, subscription already held — *but its TTFT is unverified and
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6252-  load-bearing*); (2) **Claude Haiku 4.5** via direct API (~1.04s, best TTFT consistency — needs API
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6253-  billing, not the Max sub); (3) **GPT-5.6 at `none`/`low` effort** (OpenAI's own guidance names
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6254-  `low` for latency-sensitive work — but same family as the 13s number, so verify).
docs/sol/sol_round4.out:6255:- **Explicitly NOT the head:** GPT-5.6-sol at high/max, Claude Fable, **anything behind `claude-cli`.**
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6256-- **Dispatch targets (Reasoners):** GPT-5.6-sol high/max via Codex; Claude Opus/Fable via
docs/sol/sol_round4.out:6257:  `claude-cli`; Grok Build; Ollama. All spawned non-blocking via `sessions_spawn` with explicit
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6258-  per-spawn `model`. Their multi-second startup is irrelevant in the background.
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6259-
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6260-### 🔬 Spike C (new, and it gates the architecture)
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6261-
--
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6342-
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6343-### Other things that bite
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6344-
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6345-- **Hard 60-minute session cap.** A 2-hour builder session needs ≥2 reconnects with context
docs/sol/sol_round4.out:6346:  rehydration — and a fresh session means a **cold cache** (cost spike + a memory seam). Needs
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6347-  design, not hand-waving.
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6348-- **Prompt caching is load-bearing, not an optimization.** Without it the context-replay term goes
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6349-  from $0.168/hr → **$13.44/hr**. That is the entire difference between "cheap" and "unusable."
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6350-  Requires a stable prefix — never interpolate timestamps into the system prompt.
--
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6461-|---|---|
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6462-| **PWA, background / locked screen, wake word** | 🔴 **IMPOSSIBLE.** Not hard — *impossible.* iOS suspends PWAs shortly after backgrounding; service workers get only narrow event-driven windows and **cannot** do continuous audio ([WebKit #211018](https://bugs.webkit.org/show_bug.cgi?id=211018)). State this to the owner without hedging. |
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6463-| **PWA, foreground, phone mounted, open mic** | ✅ **WORKS TODAY. This is the 90% answer.** Tap once on entering the car, then talk freely the whole drive, with barge-in. Add **Wake Lock API** to keep the screen awake. ~**a day of work, mostly deleting PTT logic.** Gotcha: use a single-page shell with **no hash routing** — iOS standalone-mode `getUserMedia` re-prompts on navigation ([WebKit #185448](https://bugs.webkit.org/show_bug.cgi?id=185448), [#215884](https://bugs.webkit.org/show_bug.cgi?id=215884)). |
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6464-| **Siri App Intent as launcher → hands off to our session** | ⭐ **THE SLEEPER. Best effort-to-payoff ratio.** *"Hey Siri, ask Chief…"* works **hands-free, screen locked, phone in pocket**, needs **no special entitlement and no wake-word engine** — Siri gives us the system-level wake word for free, then our app takes over the mic and runs the full open-mic Realtime session. Requires a thin native shell. Ceiling (inferred): Siri is a *launcher*, not a host — you don't run a Realtime session inside Siri's voice loop. |
docs/sol/sol_round4.out:6465:| **Native app + Porcupine + `UIBackgroundModes: audio`** | ✅ Achievable. True custom *"hey Chief"*, screen off, in-pocket. **Sideloading removes the App Store constraint entirely** — `UIBackgroundModes: audio` is a *normal capability*, not a restricted entitlement. No review, no rejection risk. Costs: a real native app, a wake-word integration, and a **persistent red mic dot in the status bar** (cannot be suppressed). |
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6466-| **CarPlay** | 🔴 **HARD BLOCKER. CUT IT.** iOS 26.4 *did* add exactly the right category (voice-based conversational apps, built for ChatGPT/Gemini-class assistants) — but **CarPlay entitlements are Apple-granted per-category, case-by-case, and cannot be self-signed.** No personal provisioning profile can carry one. Apple granting a one-man internal tool a conversational-AI CarPlay entitlement is ~zero. Also: Apple is explicitly *not* handing the wake-word slot to third parties — Siri stays the system assistant in CarPlay. |
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6467-
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6468-#### 🔧 Owner data point: the Archie app (real prior experience, and it corrects the above)
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6469-
--
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6498-- **[Apple Forums #776055](https://developer.apple.com/forums/thread/776055):** a *shipping* native
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6499-  background-recording app reports a subset of users **routinely get recordings killed** by the
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6500-  watchdog. They stripped the entire view hierarchy on background to get CPU to 0% in the profiler,
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6501-  saw "massive improvements," and **still see background terminations.** Apple publishes **no CPU or
docs/sol/sol_round4.out:6502:  memory thresholds** for what survives.
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6503-- **Picovoice's own FAQ hedges:** background wake-word "is controlled by the operating system, and
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6504-  Picovoice **cannot guarantee** that this will be possible in future releases of iOS."
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6505-- Every phone call, Siri invocation, or app grabbing the mic is an interruption you must catch and
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6506-  recover from — and recovery is not always granted. User swipes the app away → dead, nothing
--
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6566-  `workspace`, `tools` profile, `identity`. All four providers natively supported:
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6567-  - **Codex** — supported, but a distinct runtime path (`codex` plugin + native app-server harness). Fiddliest of the four; budget real time.
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6568-  - **Grok/xAI** — supported; SuperGrok OAuth is the documented recommended path (subject to the tier trap above).
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6569-  - **Ollama** — supported, auto-detected at `127.0.0.1:11434`.
docs/sol/sol_round4.out:6570:  - **Claude headless** — supported via `agentRuntime.id: "claude-cli"` (reuses `claude -p` under your own session auth). This is the escalation seat.
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6571-- **The dispatcher** = native subagents (`sessions_spawn`). Non-blocking, returns a run id,
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6572-  and **each spawn takes an explicit `model` param** — so one orchestrator turn fans out across
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6573-  Grok + Ollama + Claude simultaneously. `maxConcurrent` default 8; `maxSpawnDepth` 2 is the
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6574-  documented orchestrator pattern. **Do not build a custom dispatcher.**
--
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6716-## 6. Ranked recommendations
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6717-
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6718-**Adopt as-is**
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6719-- OpenClaw core, **pinned to an exact version** — seats as `agents.list[]`, `codex` plugin for the
docs/sol/sol_round4.out:6720:  orchestrator, `agentRuntime.id: "claude-cli"` for the Claude seat.
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6721-- Native subagents for dispatch (`maxSpawnDepth: 2`, per-spawn `model` override).
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6722-- `autoreview` skill as the gauntlet spine.
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6723-- `openclaw gateway install` (launchd) + Tailscale Serve auto-config.
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6724-- **Pipecat** (BSD-2) as the voice framework; `kokoro-onnx` via `pipecat-ai[kokoro]` for TTS;
--
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6742-- calimero (severity × agreement-count aggregation).
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6743-- clu (SQLite atomic claiming, checkpoints).
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6744-- `claude-to-codex` / `ccode-to-codex` — **take the mapping spec, hand-write the ~200 lines.** Both
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6745-  are experimental (`claude-to-codex` self-describes as *"primarily vibe-coded"*, 6★) and **silently
docs/sol/sol_round4.out:6746:  drop hooks, `maxTurns`, and memory settings.** Our agent definitions are the actual product —
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6747-  don't run an unvetted converter over them. Note the non-obvious effort remap
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6748-  (`high`→`xhigh`, `medium`→`high`).
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6749-
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-6750-**Ignore**
--
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-7136-- Reviewers may share blind spots or accept a request that was carried out correctly but was never genuinely intended.
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-7137-
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-7138-The compensations are uneven:
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-7139-
docs/sol/sol_round4.out:7140:- The VM and credential absence are powerful compensations. If implemented correctly, they make the harness substantially safer than running an unrestricted coding agent directly on the owner’s Mac.
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-7141-- Provider hard caps genuinely compensate for increased spending risk.
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-7142-- Resource limits genuinely compensate for overnight exhaustion attacks if the Mac remains usable during the tests.
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-7143-- The gauntlet reduces ordinary mistakes, but does not prove intent and does not contain a malicious payload.
docs/sol/sol_round4.out-7144-- The record helps discovery, explanation, and recovery after the event. It does not prevent the event.
--
