docs/sol/sol_round1.out-185-|---|---|---|
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-186-| **Different agent runners** (Claude Code, Codex, Ollama) | Yes — each has its own config; one will. | Branch protection, credential absence, sandbox, no DB write, disposable worktree. The runner that skips still holds no keys. |
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-187-| **MCP servers** an agent connects to | Yes — an MCP tool acts directly, the hook may never see it. | The MCP server runs inside the agent's sandbox and under the agent user; it inherits the same absent credentials and network allow-list. An MCP server that needs a real credential can't get one. New MCP servers are allow-listed, not auto-trusted. |
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-188-| **Agent hooks / tool wrappers** the runner itself fires | Yes | Same as runners — they execute as the sandboxed agent user. A hook can't grant itself a door. |
docs/sol/sol_round1.out:189:| **Scheduled / overnight jobs** (OpenClaw scheduler) | They run *unattended*, so a skipped check has no human nearby. | Overnight jobs get the *most* restricted profile: local/subscription seats only, no dangerous capability can be requested while unattended (approvals require a live read-back, which needs the owner awake — see §6). Worst case overnight is wasted tokens and code sitting in a branch for morning review. |
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-190-| **The voice path** | The voice holds one tool and no credentials; it can't act. | It reaches Chief, Chief reaches the harness, the harness dispatches. Nothing on the voice path can perform a dangerous action; it can only *ask*. |
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-191-| **The gatekeeper / release service itself** | It is the one thing that *must* act. | It is the smallest, most-reviewed component, runs as a separate trusted user, authenticates its callers, and re-verifies every request against records only it can write (§4). It is the single trusted computing base — kept tiny on purpose. |
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-192-| **OpenClaw upgrades** | A new version can change defaults (sandbox off, exec policy loosened) silently. | Version-pinned to `2026.6.11`; upgrades are deliberate. Every upgrade re-runs the full attack test (§8) before it's trusted, and the sandbox/exec-policy settings are asserted in a startup check that refuses to run if they've drifted. |
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-193-
--
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-366-- **Supply-chain controls.** A builder can add a package or alter build automation; both
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-367-  reach `main` only through a PR + gauntlet + branch protection, and the automation that
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-368-  holds repo secrets is itself protected config, changeable only through the gate.
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-369-- **Memory hygiene.** Lessons carry provenance (which job taught them) and can be expired
docs/sol/sol_round1.out:370:  or corrected; project memory is strictly scoped so the EMR's lessons never bleed into
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-371-  the harness.
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-372-- **A realistic driving test set** — road noise, passengers, radio speech, weak signal,
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-373-  interruptions, self-corrections, ambiguous confirmations. Built before the voice is
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-374-  trusted for anything but calm, stopped conversation.
--
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-517-- **Networking:** Tailscale mesh. The harness binds to the tailnet, never the public internet. Phone and MacBook reach it via Tailscale hostname. No port forwarding, no exposed services.
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-518-
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-519-## 3. Core harness: OpenClaw
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-520-
docs/sol/sol_round1.out:521:OpenClaw is the orchestration backbone — gateway, session management, agent memory, and the routing layer to all model providers.
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-522-
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-523-Requirements:
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-524-- Install pinned to a specific version. **No auto-updates.** Version bumps are deliberate, tested events (the Gemini CLI shutdown is the cautionary tale — vendor and tooling behavior changes under you).
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-525-- All provider connections defined in config, each swappable independently.
--
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-551-
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-552-### 4.4 Escalation reviewer — Claude (Max subscription + credit pool)
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-553-- Two modes, and the distinction matters:
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-554-  - **Interactive (subscription, effectively free):** Neill opens a Claude Code session (locally or via SSH) and reviews the gauntlet's flagged queue himself with Claude. Human-initiated = subscription limits.
docs/sol/sol_round1.out:555:  - **Headless (credit pool):** the harness may invoke `claude -p` for automated deep reviews, drawing on the Max plan's included monthly agent credits. Budget-cap this in config (e.g., N automated Claude reviews/day) so it never silently overruns the free allotment.
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-556-- Role: highest-judgment work — architecture review, contested reviewer verdicts, the bug the local model looped on three times.
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-557-- This seat is optional/droppable. The system must run fully without it.
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-558-
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-559-### 4.5 Explicitly excluded
--
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-561-
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-562-## 5. Voice layer (the product)
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-563-
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-564-### 5.1 The core architecture decision — voice skin ≠ brain
docs/sol/sol_round1.out:565:The voice layer and the orchestrator are **separate**. A fast, local voice front-end handles listening and speaking; it makes **tool calls** to the GPT-5.6 orchestrator (Codex, §4.1) which holds the harness memory, subagents, and reasoning. You are talking *to your actual orchestrator* — the local pipeline is just its ears and mouth. This is what kills the Chief Command latency wound: the voice layer acknowledges in ~300ms ("on it, kicking that off") while 5.6 reasons and builds underneath. Perceived latency is the voice layer's, not the builder's.
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-566-
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-567-This means you get 5.6's full intelligence — interrogating a build, pushing back, inspecting its own work — with local, free, low-latency voice on top. The orchestrator is never a dumbed-down voice model; it's the real brain with all the context and subagents.
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-568-
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-569-### 5.2 Interaction model
--
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-597-- Codex does not auto-spawn custom agents — delegation must be written explicitly into the workflow prompts / AGENTS.md.
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-598-
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-599-## 7. Memory & state
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-600-
docs/sol/sol_round1.out:601:- Port Neill's existing memory files (CLAUDE.md / MEMORY.md-style markdown) into a harness-level `memory/` directory loaded into orchestrator context.
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-602-- Session state, job queue, and build history in SQLite on the host.
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-603-- Every dispatched job gets a persistent record: what was asked, which provider ran it, gauntlet verdicts, final status. Voice query "what did the overnight run do?" must be answerable from this.
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-604-- Git as audit trail for everything the agents produce.
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-605-
