docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1303-- **Dispatch targets (Reasoners):** GPT-5.6-sol high/max via Codex; Claude Opus/Fable via
docs/sol/sol_round1.out:1304:  `claude-cli`; Grok Build; Ollama. All spawned non-blocking via `sessions_spawn` with explicit
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1305-  per-spawn `model`. Their multi-second startup is irrelevant in the background.
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1306-
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1307-### 🔬 Spike C (new, and it gates the architecture)
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1308-
--
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1389-
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1390-### Other things that bite
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1391-
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1392-- **Hard 60-minute session cap.** A 2-hour builder session needs ≥2 reconnects with context
docs/sol/sol_round1.out:1393:  rehydration — and a fresh session means a **cold cache** (cost spike + a memory seam). Needs
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1394-  design, not hand-waving.
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1395-- **Prompt caching is load-bearing, not an optimization.** Without it the context-replay term goes
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1396-  from $0.168/hr → **$13.44/hr**. That is the entire difference between "cheap" and "unusable."
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1397-  Requires a stable prefix — never interpolate timestamps into the system prompt.
--
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1508-|---|---|
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1509-| **PWA, background / locked screen, wake word** | 🔴 **IMPOSSIBLE.** Not hard — *impossible.* iOS suspends PWAs shortly after backgrounding; service workers get only narrow event-driven windows and **cannot** do continuous audio ([WebKit #211018](https://bugs.webkit.org/show_bug.cgi?id=211018)). State this to the owner without hedging. |
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1510-| **PWA, foreground, phone mounted, open mic** | ✅ **WORKS TODAY. This is the 90% answer.** Tap once on entering the car, then talk freely the whole drive, with barge-in. Add **Wake Lock API** to keep the screen awake. ~**a day of work, mostly deleting PTT logic.** Gotcha: use a single-page shell with **no hash routing** — iOS standalone-mode `getUserMedia` re-prompts on navigation ([WebKit #185448](https://bugs.webkit.org/show_bug.cgi?id=185448), [#215884](https://bugs.webkit.org/show_bug.cgi?id=215884)). |
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1511-| **Siri App Intent as launcher → hands off to our session** | ⭐ **THE SLEEPER. Best effort-to-payoff ratio.** *"Hey Siri, ask Chief…"* works **hands-free, screen locked, phone in pocket**, needs **no special entitlement and no wake-word engine** — Siri gives us the system-level wake word for free, then our app takes over the mic and runs the full open-mic Realtime session. Requires a thin native shell. Ceiling (inferred): Siri is a *launcher*, not a host — you don't run a Realtime session inside Siri's voice loop. |
docs/sol/sol_round1.out:1512:| **Native app + Porcupine + `UIBackgroundModes: audio`** | ✅ Achievable. True custom *"hey Chief"*, screen off, in-pocket. **Sideloading removes the App Store constraint entirely** — `UIBackgroundModes: audio` is a *normal capability*, not a restricted entitlement. No review, no rejection risk. Costs: a real native app, a wake-word integration, and a **persistent red mic dot in the status bar** (cannot be suppressed). |
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1513-| **CarPlay** | 🔴 **HARD BLOCKER. CUT IT.** iOS 26.4 *did* add exactly the right category (voice-based conversational apps, built for ChatGPT/Gemini-class assistants) — but **CarPlay entitlements are Apple-granted per-category, case-by-case, and cannot be self-signed.** No personal provisioning profile can carry one. Apple granting a one-man internal tool a conversational-AI CarPlay entitlement is ~zero. Also: Apple is explicitly *not* handing the wake-word slot to third parties — Siri stays the system assistant in CarPlay. |
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1514-
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1515-#### 🔧 Owner data point: the Archie app (real prior experience, and it corrects the above)
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1516-
--
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1545-- **[Apple Forums #776055](https://developer.apple.com/forums/thread/776055):** a *shipping* native
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1546-  background-recording app reports a subset of users **routinely get recordings killed** by the
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1547-  watchdog. They stripped the entire view hierarchy on background to get CPU to 0% in the profiler,
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1548-  saw "massive improvements," and **still see background terminations.** Apple publishes **no CPU or
docs/sol/sol_round1.out:1549:  memory thresholds** for what survives.
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1550-- **Picovoice's own FAQ hedges:** background wake-word "is controlled by the operating system, and
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1551-  Picovoice **cannot guarantee** that this will be possible in future releases of iOS."
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1552-- Every phone call, Siri invocation, or app grabbing the mic is an interruption you must catch and
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1553-  recover from — and recovery is not always granted. User swipes the app away → dead, nothing
--
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1613-  `workspace`, `tools` profile, `identity`. All four providers natively supported:
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1614-  - **Codex** — supported, but a distinct runtime path (`codex` plugin + native app-server harness). Fiddliest of the four; budget real time.
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1615-  - **Grok/xAI** — supported; SuperGrok OAuth is the documented recommended path (subject to the tier trap above).
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1616-  - **Ollama** — supported, auto-detected at `127.0.0.1:11434`.
docs/sol/sol_round1.out:1617:  - **Claude headless** — supported via `agentRuntime.id: "claude-cli"` (reuses `claude -p` under your own session auth). This is the escalation seat.
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1618-- **The dispatcher** = native subagents (`sessions_spawn`). Non-blocking, returns a run id,
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1619-  and **each spawn takes an explicit `model` param** — so one orchestrator turn fans out across
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1620-  Grok + Ollama + Claude simultaneously. `maxConcurrent` default 8; `maxSpawnDepth` 2 is the
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1621-  documented orchestrator pattern. **Do not build a custom dispatcher.**
--
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1763-## 6. Ranked recommendations
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1764-
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1765-**Adopt as-is**
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1766-- OpenClaw core, **pinned to an exact version** — seats as `agents.list[]`, `codex` plugin for the
docs/sol/sol_round1.out:1767:  orchestrator, `agentRuntime.id: "claude-cli"` for the Claude seat.
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1768-- Native subagents for dispatch (`maxSpawnDepth: 2`, per-spawn `model` override).
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1769-- `autoreview` skill as the gauntlet spine.
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1770-- `openclaw gateway install` (launchd) + Tailscale Serve auto-config.
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1771-- **Pipecat** (BSD-2) as the voice framework; `kokoro-onnx` via `pipecat-ai[kokoro]` for TTS;
--
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1789-- calimero (severity × agreement-count aggregation).
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1790-- clu (SQLite atomic claiming, checkpoints).
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1791-- `claude-to-codex` / `ccode-to-codex` — **take the mapping spec, hand-write the ~200 lines.** Both
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1792-  are experimental (`claude-to-codex` self-describes as *"primarily vibe-coded"*, 6★) and **silently
docs/sol/sol_round1.out:1793:  drop hooks, `maxTurns`, and memory settings.** Our agent definitions are the actual product —
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1794-  don't run an unvetted converter over them. Note the non-obvious effort remap
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1795-  (`high`→`xhigh`, `medium`→`high`).
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1796-
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-1797-**Ignore**
