docs/sol/sol_round1.out-6426-// reason is NOT the one in the spec.
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-6427-//
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-6428-// ⚠️ THE SPEC'S REASON IS WRONG. It says Claude headless draws from a metered credit
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-6429-// pool. It does NOT — Anthropic announced that, then PAUSED it before it took effect.
docs/sol/sol_round1.out:6430:// `claude -p` runs on normal subscription limits TODAY. Claude-as-workhorse would not
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-6431-// cost extra dollars.
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-6432-//
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-6433-// ✅ THE REAL REASON, which nobody had written down: RATE LIMITS, not cost. Running a
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-6434-// workhorse hard (unattended overnight builds, migrations, backfills) burns Max plan
--
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-6456-model_heavy    = "gpt-5.6-sol"              // auth, money, data, or it failed twice
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-6457-notes = "OpenAI sub. Shares auth with [seats.brain] — see CONCENTRATION RISK below."
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-6458-
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-6459-// The Claude builders — Riggs (backend), Finn (frontend), Nova (glue). Same lanes,
docs/sol/sol_round1.out:6460:// same memory, tiered the same way. Opus is the best coder we own (87.6% SWE-bench);
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-6461-// it is also the one most likely to burn a weekly cap, so it is EARNED like the rest.
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-6462-[seats.riggs]
docs/sol/sol_round1.out:6463:provider = "claude-cli"
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-6464-model = "claude-opus-4-8"
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-6465-family = "claude"
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-6466-tier = "subscription"
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-6467-model_light    = "claude-haiku-4-5"
--
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-6555-// because different models miss different bugs. Claude catching what GPT and Grok
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-6556-// both missed is the entire point of keeping it.
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-6557-//
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-6558-// Per spec §4.4 this seat is optional and droppable — the system must run without it.
docs/sol/sol_round1.out:6559:// Never at the mouth: claude-cli spawns a subprocess per call (3-5s).
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-6560-// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-6561-[seats.reviewer]
docs/sol/sol_round1.out:6562:provider = "claude-cli"                     // sanctioned path; subscription OAuth is NOT
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-6563-model = "claude-opus-4-8"
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-6564-tier = "subscription"                       // NOT credits — the pool was paused, never shipped
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-6565-optional = true
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-6566-max_calls_per_day = 25                      // keeps escalation from eating interactive headroom
--
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-6677-docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:133:| Seam | Can it skip the pre-action hook? | What stops harm anyway |
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-6678-docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:135:| **Different agent runners** (Claude Code, Codex, Ollama) | Yes — each has its own config; one will. | Branch protection, credential absence, sandbox, no DB write, disposable worktree. The runner that skips still holds no keys. |
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-6679-docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:136:| **MCP servers** an agent connects to | Yes — an MCP tool acts directly, the hook may never see it. | The MCP server runs inside the agent's sandbox and under the agent user; it inherits the same absent credentials and network allow-list. An MCP server that needs a real credential can't get one. New MCP servers are allow-listed, not auto-trusted. |
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-6680-docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:137:| **Agent hooks / tool wrappers** the runner itself fires | Yes | Same as runners — they execute as the sandboxed agent user. A hook can't grant itself a door. |
docs/sol/sol_round1.out:6681:docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:138:| **Scheduled / overnight jobs** (OpenClaw scheduler) | They run *unattended*, so a skipped check has no human nearby. | Overnight jobs get the *most* restricted profile: local/subscription seats only, no dangerous capability can be requested while unattended (approvals require a live read-back, which needs the owner awake — see §6). Worst case overnight is wasted tokens and code sitting in a branch for morning review. |
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-6682-docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:139:| **The voice path** | The voice holds one tool and no credentials; it can't act. | It reaches Chief, Chief reaches the harness, the harness dispatches. Nothing on the voice path can perform a dangerous action; it can only *ask*. |
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-6683-docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:140:| **The gatekeeper / release service itself** | It is the one thing that *must* act. | It is the smallest, most-reviewed component, runs as a separate trusted user, authenticates its callers, and re-verifies every request against records only it can write (§4). It is the single trusted computing base — kept tiny on purpose. |
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-6684-docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:141:| **OpenClaw upgrades** | A new version can change defaults (sandbox off, exec policy loosened) silently. | Version-pinned to `2026.6.11`; upgrades are deliberate. Every upgrade re-runs the full attack test (§8) before it's trusted, and the sandbox/exec-policy settings are asserted in a startup check that refuses to run if they've drifted. |
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-6685-docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:144:all at the hook. We close them at the doors, so a leaky seam is harmless.**
--
