docs/sol/sol_round1.out-7547-3. A clean build-and-release chain. The exact reviewed source must produce an identified package, and that exact package—not a rebuilt approximation—must be deployed.
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-7548-
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-7549-4. Protection against malicious code that passes model review. High-risk changes such as dependencies, deployment instructions, authentication, billing, database changes, and the release service itself need a qualified technical human review or must be prohibited from automatic release.
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-7550-
docs/sol/sol_round1.out:7551:5. Protection of private information other than credentials. There is no rule controlling what source code, customer data, memory, or transcripts may be sent to each model provider.
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-7552-
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-7553-6. Exact GitHub protections for branches, tags, automation, packages, releases, administrative bypass, and production environments.
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-7554-
docs/sol/sol_round1.out:7555:7. Resource limits for processor use, memory, disk, child processes, execution time, and network volume.
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-7556-
docs/sol/sol_round1.out:7557:8. Tested backups and restoration for the safety record, job history, project memory, configuration, and approval history. This was identified in the previous review and disappeared from the corrected architecture.
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-7558-
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-7559-9. Tamper-resistant audit records stored somewhere other than the same Mac and same trusted account.
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-7560-
docs/sol/sol_round1.out:7561:10. Incident response: emergency credential revocation, device loss, compromised phone sessions, poisoned project memory, and compromised release-service recovery.
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-7562-
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-7563-11. Protection of the reviewers themselves from instructions hidden in code, test output, issues, web pages, and dependencies.
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-7564-
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-7565-12. Project sensitivity rules. Medical information and ordinary source code cannot share the same provider and network policy merely because both are “inside a sandbox.”
--
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-7587-7. **Broker all model and provider access.** Agent runners receive no reusable subscription or API credentials. The broker applies per-job limits before every call. Hard provider-side caps must exist for every metered provider.
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-7588-
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-7589-8. **Add an enforced data-egress policy.** Identify what each project may send to each provider. Block customer, medical, private, and cross-project information independently of credential scanning.
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-7590-
docs/sol/sol_round1.out:7591:9. **Specify and test the full containment profile.** Include every runner, child process, MCP server, hook, scheduled job, retry, and recovery path. Add processor, memory, disk, process, time, and network limits.
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-7592-
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-7593-10. **Replace the voice flow with a deterministic turn controller.** No dispatch from partial speech; authoritative acknowledgements only; exact warnings without paraphrasing; a separate emergency-stop path; structured state outside conversation history; reconnection and long-session tests.
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-7594-
docs/sol/sol_round1.out-7595-11. **Add off-machine backups, tamper-resistant audit records, restoration drills, credential-revocation procedures, and device-loss handling.**
--
