docs/sol/sol_family_floor_task10.out-236-- **Networking:** Tailscale mesh. The harness binds to the tailnet, never the public internet. Phone and MacBook reach it via Tailscale hostname. No port forwarding, no exposed services.
docs/sol/sol_family_floor_task10.out-237-
docs/sol/sol_family_floor_task10.out-238-## 3. Core harness: OpenClaw
docs/sol/sol_family_floor_task10.out-239-
docs/sol/sol_family_floor_task10.out:240:OpenClaw is the orchestration backbone — gateway, session management, agent memory, and the routing layer to all model providers.
docs/sol/sol_family_floor_task10.out-241-
docs/sol/sol_family_floor_task10.out-242-Requirements:
docs/sol/sol_family_floor_task10.out-243-- Install pinned to a specific version. **No auto-updates.** Version bumps are deliberate, tested events (the Gemini CLI shutdown is the cautionary tale — vendor and tooling behavior changes under you).
docs/sol/sol_family_floor_task10.out-244-- All provider connections defined in config, each swappable independently.
--
docs/sol/sol_family_floor_task10.out-270-
docs/sol/sol_family_floor_task10.out-271-### 4.4 Escalation reviewer — Claude (Max subscription + credit pool)
docs/sol/sol_family_floor_task10.out-272-- Two modes, and the distinction matters:
docs/sol/sol_family_floor_task10.out-273-  - **Interactive (subscription, effectively free):** Neill opens a Claude Code session (locally or via SSH) and reviews the gauntlet's flagged queue himself with Claude. Human-initiated = subscription limits.
docs/sol/sol_family_floor_task10.out:274:  - **Headless (credit pool):** the harness may invoke `claude -p` for automated deep reviews, drawing on the Max plan's included monthly agent credits. Budget-cap this in config (e.g., N automated Claude reviews/day) so it never silently overruns the free allotment.
docs/sol/sol_family_floor_task10.out-275-- Role: highest-judgment work — architecture review, contested reviewer verdicts, the bug the local model looped on three times.
docs/sol/sol_family_floor_task10.out-276-- This seat is optional/droppable. The system must run fully without it.
docs/sol/sol_family_floor_task10.out-277-
docs/sol/sol_family_floor_task10.out-278-### 4.5 Explicitly excluded
--
docs/sol/sol_family_floor_task10.out-280-
docs/sol/sol_family_floor_task10.out-281-## 5. Voice layer (the product)
docs/sol/sol_family_floor_task10.out-282-
docs/sol/sol_family_floor_task10.out-283-### 5.1 The core architecture decision — voice skin ≠ brain
docs/sol/sol_family_floor_task10.out:284:The voice layer and the orchestrator are **separate**. A fast, local voice front-end handles listening and speaking; it makes **tool calls** to the GPT-5.6 orchestrator (Codex, §4.1) which holds the harness memory, subagents, and reasoning. You are talking *to your actual orchestrator* — the local pipeline is just its ears and mouth. This is what kills the Chief Command latency wound: the voice layer acknowledges in ~300ms ("on it, kicking that off") while 5.6 reasons and builds underneath. Perceived latency is the voice layer's, not the builder's.
docs/sol/sol_family_floor_task10.out-285-
docs/sol/sol_family_floor_task10.out-286-This means you get 5.6's full intelligence — interrogating a build, pushing back, inspecting its own work — with local, free, low-latency voice on top. The orchestrator is never a dumbed-down voice model; it's the real brain with all the context and subagents.
docs/sol/sol_family_floor_task10.out-287-
docs/sol/sol_family_floor_task10.out-288-### 5.2 Interaction model
--
docs/sol/sol_family_floor_task10.out-316-- Codex does not auto-spawn custom agents — delegation must be written explicitly into the workflow prompts / AGENTS.md.
docs/sol/sol_family_floor_task10.out-317-
docs/sol/sol_family_floor_task10.out-318-## 7. Memory & state
docs/sol/sol_family_floor_task10.out-319-
docs/sol/sol_family_floor_task10.out:320:- Port Neill's existing memory files (CLAUDE.md / MEMORY.md-style markdown) into a harness-level `memory/` directory loaded into orchestrator context.
docs/sol/sol_family_floor_task10.out-321-- Session state, job queue, and build history in SQLite on the host.
docs/sol/sol_family_floor_task10.out-322-- Every dispatched job gets a persistent record: what was asked, which provider ran it, gauntlet verdicts, final status. Voice query "what did the overnight run do?" must be answerable from this.
docs/sol/sol_family_floor_task10.out-323-- Git as audit trail for everything the agents produce.
docs/sol/sol_family_floor_task10.out-324-
