docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-40-=== 6. CAPABILITY GATES (built after your last review) ===
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-41-A table of what each agent may do. Safe: read code, write in own worktree, run tests, open a PR. Denied by default: touch production, delete data, deploy, merge to main, force push, read secrets, send external requests, spend money. Anything irreversible needs a single-use approval naming the exact action, with what was READ BACK ALOUD to Neill, and a recovery plan.
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-42-
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-43-=== 7. THE FLAW I HAVE ALREADY FOUND MYSELF ===
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt:44:I now believe the capability gates are theatre. They gate the RECORD, not the ACTION. A database row saying "agents may not touch production" is a piece of paper — if the agent has a shell, it CAN touch production and the database simply never finds out. I built a permissions table, not a permission system. Real containment would have to be at the execution layer (sandbox, no credentials, no network to prod, restricted filesystem).
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-45-
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-46-=== 8. OPEN QUESTION: OPENCLAW ===
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-47-Considering a hybrid: use OpenClaw (a popular open-source agent platform) as the execution ENGINE (parallel non-blocking dispatch, scheduling, sessions, daemon) and keep our SQLite layer as the safety/record gates on top.
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-48-
--
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-143-- **Networking:** Tailscale mesh. The harness binds to the tailnet, never the public internet. Phone and MacBook reach it via Tailscale hostname. No port forwarding, no exposed services.
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-144-
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-145-## 3. Core harness: OpenClaw
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-146-
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt:147:OpenClaw is the orchestration backbone — gateway, session management, agent memory, and the routing layer to all model providers.
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-148-
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-149-Requirements:
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-150-- Install pinned to a specific version. **No auto-updates.** Version bumps are deliberate, tested events (the Gemini CLI shutdown is the cautionary tale — vendor and tooling behavior changes under you).
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-151-- All provider connections defined in config, each swappable independently.
--
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-177-
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-178-### 4.4 Escalation reviewer — Claude (Max subscription + credit pool)
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-179-- Two modes, and the distinction matters:
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-180-  - **Interactive (subscription, effectively free):** Neill opens a Claude Code session (locally or via SSH) and reviews the gauntlet's flagged queue himself with Claude. Human-initiated = subscription limits.
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt:181:  - **Headless (credit pool):** the harness may invoke `claude -p` for automated deep reviews, drawing on the Max plan's included monthly agent credits. Budget-cap this in config (e.g., N automated Claude reviews/day) so it never silently overruns the free allotment.
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-182-- Role: highest-judgment work — architecture review, contested reviewer verdicts, the bug the local model looped on three times.
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-183-- This seat is optional/droppable. The system must run fully without it.
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-184-
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-185-### 4.5 Explicitly excluded
--
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-187-
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-188-## 5. Voice layer (the product)
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-189-
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-190-### 5.1 The core architecture decision — voice skin ≠ brain
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt:191:The voice layer and the orchestrator are **separate**. A fast, local voice front-end handles listening and speaking; it makes **tool calls** to the GPT-5.6 orchestrator (Codex, §4.1) which holds the harness memory, subagents, and reasoning. You are talking *to your actual orchestrator* — the local pipeline is just its ears and mouth. This is what kills the Chief Command latency wound: the voice layer acknowledges in ~300ms ("on it, kicking that off") while 5.6 reasons and builds underneath. Perceived latency is the voice layer's, not the builder's.
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-192-
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-193-This means you get 5.6's full intelligence — interrogating a build, pushing back, inspecting its own work — with local, free, low-latency voice on top. The orchestrator is never a dumbed-down voice model; it's the real brain with all the context and subagents.
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-194-
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-195-### 5.2 Interaction model
--
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-223-- Codex does not auto-spawn custom agents — delegation must be written explicitly into the workflow prompts / AGENTS.md.
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-224-
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-225-## 7. Memory & state
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-226-
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt:227:- Port Neill's existing memory files (CLAUDE.md / MEMORY.md-style markdown) into a harness-level `memory/` directory loaded into orchestrator context.
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-228-- Session state, job queue, and build history in SQLite on the host.
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-229-- Every dispatched job gets a persistent record: what was asked, which provider ran it, gauntlet verdicts, final status. Voice query "what did the overnight run do?" must be answerable from this.
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-230-- Git as audit trail for everything the agents produce.
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-231-
--
