docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-628-  defaults stay cheap because heavy lifting moves to layer 3.
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-629-
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-630-### Risks
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-631-
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt:632:- **Prompt injection from memory files.** Memory files are concatenated into
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-633-  the system prompt. A compromised sub-agent writing to one could hijack
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-634-  Chief's behavior. Mitigation: identity-prompt guard text, provenance
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt:635:  fences around each file (`<memory file="…" mtime="…">…</memory>`), and
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-636-  file-source tagging so odd instructions are visible.
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt:637:- **Symlink read.** Someone dropping a symlink at `~/.claude/.../memory/`
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-638-  to `~/.ssh/id_rsa` would get it read into Chief's context and sent to
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-639-  Anthropic. Mitigation: symlink rejection at read time (implemented in
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt:640:  `memory_paths.safe_md_files` + `team_service`/`memory_service`).
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-641-
docs/sol/sol_out2.txt-642----
