docs/sol/sol_round3.out-16-Since your round-5 rejection, the document has been rewritten to implement your required changes:
docs/sol/sol_round3.out-17-- Decision A revised: a qualified independent technical human must inspect high-authority changes; the owner's authenticated (device-bound, single-use, digest-bound) written approval is only the business authorization. The reviewer seat is currently EMPTY and the system FAILS CLOSED — high-authority changes queue unreleased until a qualified reviewer exists. The owner accepted this reduces autonomous scope.
docs/sol/sol_round3.out-18-- Classification is by production authority with fail-closed intake (binaries, generated output, dependencies including lockfile-only and transitive, build behavior, runtime-interpreted config, links, sub-project pointers), cumulative since the last human review, with the classifier itself always high-authority.
docs/sol/sol_round3.out-19-- Production containment: per-component enumeration of data/records/secrets/destinations/spend, enforced outside the component; components without an enumeration are high-authority.
docs/sol/sol_round3.out:20:- The release chain: restricted patch intake (never a built package), a clean builder with pinned dependency digests, signed origin in an immutable store, production-side signature verification making the path exclusive, separated powers (merge caller / builder / signer / deployer / spend broker / record-writer as distinct identities), limited rollout, independent post-release monitoring, a production kill switch distinct from the job stop, rollback that cannot revive a withdrawn package.
docs/sol/sol_round3.out-21-- Egress as architecture with admitted limits; the VM profile completed per your list (no sharing at all, fixed disk, broker-only egress, host-side firewall, no Tailscale identity inside, residual same-Mac risk stated); brokers with exact authority and hostile-input rules including PR-triggered automation; authenticated approval and fixed-playback delivery of critical text; secret lifecycle; realistic update policy; explicit decommissioning of every legacy path you found in the current code; and the acceptance test rewritten as 14 explicit fail-closed attack cases plus named residual risks.
