docs/sol/sol_arch_openclaw_v2.txt-1-You are advising a NON-TECHNICAL owner (Neill) on an architecture decision. He cannot read code. Write your answer in PLAIN ENGLISH for him — no jargon, no filenames, no bullets-as-crutch. Be blunt and specific. Your job is to find what's wrong with the reasoning, not to be encouraging.
docs/sol/sol_arch_openclaw_v2.txt-2-
docs/sol/sol_arch_openclaw_v2.txt-3-THE SITUATION
docs/sol/sol_arch_openclaw_v2.txt-4-
docs/sol/sol_arch_openclaw_v2.txt:5:Neill is building a voice-first system that dispatches AI coding agents to build software for him. He talks to it while driving. The original plan was to build on OPENCLAW — a popular open-source agent platform (very widely used; provides a persistent daemon, multi-provider model routing, parallel non-blocking agent spawning, session management, agent memory, a plugin ecosystem, a web UI, and Tailscale/launchd integration).
docs/sol/sol_arch_openclaw_v2.txt-6-
docs/sol/sol_arch_openclaw_v2.txt:7:Over about 24 hours, his AI assistant (Claude) instead built a PARALLEL system: a SQLite job store with ~24 database triggers that enforce quality gates, plus its own dispatch (shelling out to CLI tools), its own web UI, its own memory (per-project and per-lane), its own budget caps, and capability gates.
docs/sol/sol_arch_openclaw_v2.txt-8-
docs/sol/sol_arch_openclaw_v2.txt-9-WHAT THE CUSTOM LAYER ENFORCES (these are database triggers — the write physically fails):
docs/sol/sol_arch_openclaw_v2.txt-10-- A job built by the free local model cannot be marked done without a passing review from a paid-tier model
docs/sol/sol_arch_openclaw_v2.txt-11-- A tester cannot record a "pass" without real captured browser screenshots — "I ran it and it worked" is unsayable
--
docs/sol/sol_arch_openclaw_v2.txt-17-
docs/sol/sol_arch_openclaw_v2.txt-18-WHAT OPENCLAW HAS THAT THE CUSTOM LAYER DOESN'T:
docs/sol/sol_arch_openclaw_v2.txt-19-- Parallel, non-blocking agent dispatch (fire 8 agents, get IDs back instantly). The custom layer shells out to CLI tools synchronously.
docs/sol/sol_arch_openclaw_v2.txt-20-- Scheduled/cron autonomy (overnight runs)
docs/sol/sol_arch_openclaw_v2.txt:21:- Battle-tested session management and memory
docs/sol/sol_arch_openclaw_v2.txt-22-- A plugin ecosystem
docs/sol/sol_arch_openclaw_v2.txt-23-- Thousands of users finding its bugs
docs/sol/sol_arch_openclaw_v2.txt-24-
docs/sol/sol_arch_openclaw_v2.txt-25-NEILL'S SKEPTICISM (verbatim): "openclaw is very popular so I find it hard to believe that in 24 hrs we built something better."
--
docs/gpt/GPT_TASK_QUEUE.md-30-Task: have the xai runner return the token counts it got back, and have `_review_one`
docs/gpt/GPT_TASK_QUEUE.md-31-write a `usage` row (role='review', input/output tokens, cost in cents from the
docs/gpt/GPT_TASK_QUEUE.md-32-pricing pinned in `seats.toml` — add a `price_in_per_million` / `price_out_per_million`
docs/gpt/GPT_TASK_QUEUE.md-33-pair to the grok seat rather than hardcoding). The runner signature is shared with the
docs/gpt/GPT_TASK_QUEUE.md:34:CLI runners, so change it in a way that keeps `claude-cli` and `codex` working (they
docs/gpt/GPT_TASK_QUEUE.md-35-can report zero). Tests: a stubbed response with usage → a usage row with the right
docs/gpt/GPT_TASK_QUEUE.md-36-cents; a stubbed response with no usage block → a row with zeros, never a crash. Do
docs/gpt/GPT_TASK_QUEUE.md-37-NOT touch the schema triggers or migrations.
docs/gpt/GPT_TASK_QUEUE.md-38-
--
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-127-   A poisoned instruction can put private material into a legitimate model request; a
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-128-   narrow domain allow-list doesn't help when the allowed destination is itself an exfil
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-129-   channel. (PHI in the EMR makes this non-optional.)
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-130-9. **Specify and test the full containment profile** — every runner, child process, MCP
docs/sol/sol_round2.out:131:   server, hook, scheduled job, retry, recovery path — plus CPU/memory/disk/process/
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-132-   time/network limits. (An agent can brick the Mac by exhaustion; a budget cap stops none
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-133-   of that.)
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-134-10. **Replace the voice flow with a deterministic turn controller.** No dispatch from
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-135-    partial speech; authoritative acknowledgements only; exact warnings without voice-model
--
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-159-  hold their own credentials; a startup check is itself configuration the same compromise
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-160-  can disable.
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-161-- **Ambient speakers:** phone auth proves which phone opened the session, not who's
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-162-  talking. A passenger, radio, or podcast can issue a routine dispatch or a stop.
docs/sol/sol_round2.out:163:- **Private information beyond credentials** (source, business plans, issues, memory,
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-164-  customer/medical data) has no egress rule at all.
