docs/sol/sol_round2.out-293-  deleting its folder wouldn't undo it. Each agent gets a separate clone or a copy-on-write
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-294-  snapshot with no path back to the real repo.
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-295-- **A full containment profile, specified and tested**, covering every runner (Claude Code,
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-296-  Codex, Ollama), every child process, every MCP server, every hook, every scheduled job,
docs/sol/sol_round2.out:297:  every retry and recovery path — plus hard **resource limits**: CPU, memory, disk,
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-298-  child-process count, execution time, network volume. An agent can brick the Mac by
docs/sol/sol_round2.out:299:  exhaustion (fill the disk, fork-bomb, exhaust memory); a spend cap stops none of that.
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-300-- **The kernel sandbox on, strict**: deny credential paths (`~/.ssh`, `~/.aws`, keychain),
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-301-  scrub credential env vars, narrow the domain allow-list (never a bare `github.com`
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-302-  wildcard — domain fronting), `allowUnsandboxedCommands: false`. Noted honestly: a startup
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-303-  check that asserts these is itself configuration; it detects drift, it is not independent
--
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-396-
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-397-## Data egress — protecting more than credentials
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-398-
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-399-The first design guarded credentials and ignored everything else worth protecting: private
docs/sol/sol_round2.out:400:source, business plans, issue contents, conversation history, project memory, customer
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-401-data, and **medical data in other projects**. An agent must be allowed to talk to its model
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-402-provider — so a poisoned instruction can stuff private material into a *legitimate* model
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-403-request, and a narrow domain allow-list doesn't help when the allowed destination is itself
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-404-a channel that carries data out.
--
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-464-## Resilience — backups, audit, incident response
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-465-
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-466-Round 4 restored items that fell out of the prior draft:
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-467-
docs/sol/sol_round2.out:468:- **Off-machine backups** of the safety record, job history, project memory, config, and
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-469-  approval history — with **tested restoration drills**, not just backups that exist.
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-470-- **Tamper-resistant audit records** stored off this Mac and off the trusted account, so a
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-471-  compromise of either can't quietly rewrite history.
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-472-- **Incident response**: emergency credential revocation, device-loss handling, compromised
docs/sol/sol_round2.out:473:  phone-session handling, poisoned-memory recovery, and release-service compromise recovery.
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-474-- **Crash recovery**: on startup, reconcile any `in_progress` job with a dead run id — never
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-475-  silently lost — and ensure a stale approval can't remain live when unattended work
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-476-  resumes.
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-477-
--
