docs/sol/sol_round2.out:3942:- A tested response to disk filling, memory pressure, network flooding and VM crashes.
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-3943-
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-3944-Even then, the VM and the business remain in one physical failure domain. A separate machine is materially safer. The owner may accept the residual risk of a VM, but the document must state that this protects mainly against ordinary containment failures; it does not make hostile execution on the personal Mac risk-free.
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-3945-
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-3946-## 7. Missing entirely
--
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-3986-1. **Restore the actual human-review requirement.** High-authority changes require review by a qualified, independent technical human. Neill separately authorizes the business decision. If no qualified reviewer is available, those changes do not release.
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-3987-2. **Classify by production authority, not filenames.** Any change to a component with valuable production access is dangerous by default. Autonomous release is limited to components whose runtime authority is already harmless. Classification covers cumulative changes since the last human review.
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-3988-3. **Define production containment.** State exactly what every deployed component can read, write, spend, contact and reveal. This is the missing boundary against code that passes review.
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-3989-4. **Make the release path truly exclusive.** Production accepts only packages signed through this chain. Disable all old deployment scripts, automation identities, administrator bypasses and alternate routes.
docs/sol/sol_round2.out:3990:5. **Define the trusted package chain completely.** The agent submits only a restricted patch—not a built package. A clean builder creates the package, records signed origin, stores it immutably, and production verifies the signature and fingerprint.
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-3991-6. **Replace the egress slogan with enforceable architecture.** Put network enforcement outside the agent and production workloads. Define which project information may deliberately be sent to which provider. Admit that an allowed provider can receive allowed private source; do not claim that generic scanning can prevent all encoded exfiltration.
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-3992-7. **Define every broker’s exact authority and hostile-input rules.** Include GitHub automation, remote tool servers, voice, Chief, reviewers and scheduled work.
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-3993-8. **Complete the VM profile.** No host or local-network reachability, no shared devices, fixed resources, broker-only outgoing traffic, and an explicit statement of same-Mac residual risk.
docs/sol/sol_round2.out-3994-9. **Define authenticated human approval and exact critical-message delivery.** Critical warnings are played from fixed text, not paraphrased. Written approval is device-bound, short-lived, single-use and tied to the exact package and action.
--
