docs/sol/sol_prompt.txt-27-=== 6. CAPABILITY GATES (built after your last review) ===
docs/sol/sol_prompt.txt-28-A table of what each agent may do. Safe: read code, write in own worktree, run tests, open a PR. Denied by default: touch production, delete data, deploy, merge to main, force push, read secrets, send external requests, spend money. Anything irreversible needs a single-use approval naming the exact action, with what was READ BACK ALOUD to Neill, and a recovery plan.
docs/sol/sol_prompt.txt-29-
docs/sol/sol_prompt.txt-30-=== 7. THE FLAW I HAVE ALREADY FOUND MYSELF ===
docs/sol/sol_prompt.txt:31:I now believe the capability gates are theatre. They gate the RECORD, not the ACTION. A database row saying "agents may not touch production" is a piece of paper — if the agent has a shell, it CAN touch production and the database simply never finds out. I built a permissions table, not a permission system. Real containment would have to be at the execution layer (sandbox, no credentials, no network to prod, restricted filesystem).
docs/sol/sol_prompt.txt-32-
docs/sol/sol_prompt.txt-33-=== 8. OPEN QUESTION: OPENCLAW ===
docs/sol/sol_prompt.txt-34-Considering a hybrid: use OpenClaw (a popular open-source agent platform) as the execution ENGINE (parallel non-blocking dispatch, scheduling, sessions, daemon) and keep our SQLite layer as the safety/record gates on top.
docs/sol/sol_prompt.txt-35-
