codex
The plan is not safe for unattended execution or automatic merging tonight. The diff hash is not the main problem. The hard failures are that unreviewed code and its tests run as the owner, linked worktrees share Git’s control plane with `main`, and the reviewers themselves inherit dangerous tools and credentials.

## Q1. Reviewed-diff binding

The premise is slightly wrong today: `head_version` is not the full commit SHA. The executor truncates it to 16 hexadecimal characters, and the gatekeeper accepts a prefix match ([executor.py:89](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/executor.py:89), [gatekeeper.py:350](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/gatekeeper.py:350)). That is probably collision-resistant enough accidentally, but it is not the claimed exact binding. Store the full object ID.

With a full commit ID, the SHA already binds the commit’s complete tree and parent graph. Hashing a diff does not strengthen that binding. Its real purpose is different: proving that the projection shown to reviewers faithfully represented that commit. The current system needs such a check because the version and reviewed text are independent inputs ([executor.py:212](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/executor.py:212), [executor.py:227](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/executor.py:227)).

Therefore:

- SHA comparison answers: “Is this still the same commit?”
- Bundle comparison answers: “Did reviewers see an adequate and authentic rendering of that commit?”
- A hash of an unstable, incomplete rendering merely authenticates the incompleteness.

Specific under-representation cases:

| Mechanism | What reviewers may miss |
|---|---|
| Binary files | Normal `git diff` says only that binary files differ. Arbitrary payload bytes ship unseen. `--binary` makes a transport patch, not a meaningful security review. |
| Submodules/gitlinks | The diff normally shows an old and new commit ID, not the referenced repository content. A builder can create mode `160000` without a pre-existing `.gitmodules`. Ban gitlinks initially. |
| Symlinks | Git shows mode `120000` and the target string, not what that target resolves to. A merged symlink can make later tests or tools read or overwrite files outside the repository. |
| Executable/mode changes | Git does show `old mode/new mode`, but this is easy for a prose reviewer to miss. A separate path/mode/blob manifest must make it explicit. |
| `.gitattributes` | `-diff`, custom diff drivers, `textconv`, encoding rules, and LFS filters can suppress, transform, or replace what reviewers see. An LFS pointer can be reviewed while external content later appears in the checkout. |
| Shared `.git/config` | This is worse than under-representation: it is an out-of-band mutation absent from every commit and diff. A linked-worktree builder can poison diff drivers, merge drivers, hooks, filters, and Git behavior before the gatekeeper runs. |
| Rename detection | `-M` can compress a delete/add into a rename plus partial hunks. It is tree-equivalent, but rendering and reviewer perception become heuristic and configuration-dependent. Pin `--no-renames` or one exact policy. |
| CRLF and checkout conversion | Tree-to-tree diff compares committed blobs, so `core.autocrlf` is less dangerous than claimed for the committed tree. But checkout bytes may differ through EOL, encoding, filter, or attribute conversion. Blob IDs must be authoritative; test the exact checkout policy used in production. |
| No common history | `merge-base` failure must be a hard refusal. Substituting the empty tree changes the review from “topic delta” into “entire repository” and still does not make unrelated-history merging safe. |
| Merge commits in the job | Endpoint diff omits intermediate commits and second-parent history. Content added and later removed is absent from the final tree diff but becomes reachable from `main` after merging, including accidentally committed secrets. |
| Branch merges `main` | The latest common ancestor can advance, and second-parent history is hidden. Conflict-resolution changes appear only as net tree changes; the history and integration decision do not. Forbid merge commits in the first version. |
| Two-dot versus three-dot | `git diff A..B` is simply endpoint tree comparison. `git diff main...tip` compares `merge-base(main,tip)` to `tip`. If `base` is already the exact merge base, `base..tip` and `base...tip` are equivalent. `main..tip` is not: it includes inverse changes from `main` that the topic does not contain. |

The largest omission is the prospective merge result. The gatekeeper performs a real three-way merge but never verifies the resulting tree against a reviewed tree ([gatekeeper.py:284](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/gatekeeper.py:284), [gatekeeper.py:305](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/gatekeeper.py:305)). Custom merge drivers or new changes on `main` can produce a tree the panel never saw.

## Q2. Merge-base drift

I disagree with the premise that every ordinary advance of `main` changes the merge base. If `main` advances normally and the fixed topic tip has not incorporated those commits, the merge base usually remains the original branch point.

That does not make the plan safe. It exposes a worse failure:

- The stored diff can compare equal.
- The merge base can remain unchanged.
- Yet merging into the newer `main` can conflict semantically or produce a different integrated result.

Record, immutably:

- Full base commit ID.
- Full tip commit ID and tip tree ID.
- Canonical bundle digest.
- Exact renderer version/options.
- Complete path/mode/blob manifest.
- Prospective merge-tree ID against the exact `main` tip being integrated.

At merge time, either `main` must still equal the recorded integration tip, or the system must generate a new prospective merge tree, run the tests against it, and review it as a new version. Recomputing against a dynamic merge base is the wrong invariant.

## Q3. Mode selection

The mode must be immutable job history, not inferred from the current seat provider.

A job needs an explicit contract such as `text-result` or `git-tree`, fixed at dispatch before the builder starts. Better still, use an immutable reviewed-bundle record containing mode, base, tip, tree, manifest digest, rendered digest, size, and renderer identity. Every verdict should cite that bundle, not merely the tip SHA.

This cannot safely be derived later from `seats.provider`:

- The executor selects the runner using the seat’s live provider ([executor.py:176](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/executor.py:176)).
- Server synchronization rewrites provider/model/family/tier from configuration ([dispatch.py:262](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/dispatch.py:262), [jobs.py:95](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/db/jobs.py:95)).
- The existing identity guard freezes seat/tier/family but not provider, resolved model, runner type, or review mode ([schema.sql:677](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/db/schema.sql:677)).
- `jobs.result` is not frozen; ordinary status updates can replace it even after review ([jobs.py:224](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/db/jobs.py:224)).

The mode and bundle must be unchangeable after insertion—and certainly after the first verdict. Snapshot the actual provider, resolved model, CLI version, and runner contract too.

## Q4. Family arithmetic

A Claude-built job has exactly two diversity-contributing families: GPT and Grok. The Claude reviewer is still required as a seat, but is excluded from the diversity count ([schema.sql:895](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/db/schema.sql:895)). The configured roster and floor are three seats and two non-builder families ([seats.toml:357](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/config/seats.toml:357)).

Operational behavior:

- Missing Grok key before dispatch: Grok is excluded by readiness checking ([gauntlet.py:211](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/gauntlet.py:211)). After excluding the Claude builder family, only GPT remains, so dispatch is refused ([dispatch.py:240](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/dispatch.py:240)).
- Grok already capped at dispatch: the roster check does not inspect its role cap. The Claude build can run, then Grok skips during review ([gauntlet.py:368](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/gauntlet.py:368)). The job parks with only one qualifying family ([gauntlet.py:536](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/gauntlet.py:536)).
- Grok becomes capped or loses its key after dispatch: same parked result.
- Even if the family floor somehow passed, the full-panel guard requires every stamped reviewer seat to pass ([schema.sql:865](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/db/schema.sql:865)).

There is also a retry bug: `_review_one` checks current budget before looking for an already-recorded verdict. A prior Grok pass is ignored on a retry if Grok is now capped ([gauntlet.py:368](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/gauntlet.py:368), [gauntlet.py:377](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/gauntlet.py:377)).

A Claude-family tester would be rejected for a Claude build ([schema.sql:847](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/db/schema.sql:847)), leaving no acceptable tester and causing shipping to fail ([schema.sql:1000](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/db/schema.sql:1000)).

The in-flight task-4 implementation does choose the first different-family reviewer, so it selects GPT for a Claude build ([in-flight tester.py:109](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command-worktrees/gpt-merge-loop/harness/tester.py:109)). That arithmetic is compatible. Its testing semantics are not; see Q9.

Conclusion: Claude builds work only while all three reviewers are healthy and Grok has both credentials and reserved capacity. That is zero operational slack and is not suitable for an unattended night. A reliable roster or provider-level fallback must land first.

## Q5. Prompt injection into the panel

Passing the prompt as a `subprocess` argument list is not shell injection. Builder text cannot create new command-line arguments because no shell splitting occurs.

The real attacks are:

1. Semantic prompt injection: malicious comments or strings in the diff tell the reviewer to ignore the review and answer `PASS`.
2. Parser confusion: any output line beginning with `PASS`, including unintended prefixes, counts ([gauntlet.py:79](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/gauntlet.py:79)).
3. CLI echo/log confusion: arbitrary stdout is searched backwards rather than parsing one trusted result field.
4. Reviewer tool abuse: Claude and Codex reviewers currently inherit their working directory, environment, and default tools ([gauntlet.py:113](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/gauntlet.py:113), [gauntlet.py:136](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/gauntlet.py:136)). An injected diff can ask a reviewer to modify `main`, read secrets, or send them elsewhere.

Minimal structural fix:

- Reviewers run with no file-edit, shell, web, MCP, or inherited settings; preferably in a separate sandbox.
- Instructions occupy the highest available prompt channel; the bundle is clearly untrusted data.
- Each runner returns one schema-constrained object containing exact enum `pass|fail`, reason, bundle digest, reviewer identity, and invocation ID.
- Parse only the structured final-result field, never arbitrary stdout.
- Require exact equality, not `startswith`.

A nonce helps reject stale or echoed responses. It does not defeat prompt injection because the model can see the nonce. JSON output fixes parsing, not a reviewer persuaded to return a valid but dishonest `pass`.

## Q6. Environment and credentials

I am UNSURE of the exact minimum environment for this pinned Claude Code build on macOS. Likely requirements are a controlled `HOME`, temporary-directory variables, locale, user identity, and either a fixed executable path or minimal `PATH`; OAuth may additionally depend on the logged-in user’s Keychain session.

The important answer is architectural: environment stripping is useful hygiene but is not a security boundary.

It removes accidentally inherited API keys. It does not prevent a same-user process from reading the files those variables would have named, accessing the Keychain, opening the live record, reading the gatekeeper token, or altering shared Git state. The gatekeeper already admits that same-user file access means its wall does not yet exist ([gatekeeper.py:41](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/gatekeeper.py:41)).

The real boundary is:

- Dedicated OS identity, container, or VM.
- Dedicated minimal home and separately authenticated Claude session.
- Standalone clone with its own `.git`, not a linked worktree.
- Only that clone mounted writable.
- No access to the owner’s home, live record, gatekeeper socket/token, other repositories, or Keychain items.
- Network restricted to the provider endpoints actually required.
- Tests executed inside the same boundary.

If subscription authentication cannot operate inside that boundary, then the subscription builder is not safe for unattended use.

## Q7. Scale and truncation

A legitimate bundle above 200,000 characters is truncated and invited to receive a FAIL ([gauntlet.py:76](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/gauntlet.py:76), [gauntlet.py:126](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/gauntlet.py:126)). Once recorded, retries reuse that FAIL for the same version instead of rerunning ([gauntlet.py:377](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/gauntlet.py:377)). The only escape is changing the commit, even though size—not correctness—was the problem.

That is fail-closed but operationally unrecoverable. Worse, a reviewer that ignores the truncation warning might pass a partial review.

Required behavior:

- Preflight size before spending money or writing verdicts.
- Treat oversized work as “not reviewed,” never as a substantive FAIL.
- For tonight, reject it and require a smaller build.
- Longer-term, use complete file/hunk chunks plus a manifest. Certification requires coverage of every path/blob by every required reviewer, followed by an overview pass for cross-file interactions.

## Q8. Ordering and scope

The smallest safe overnight version is candidate generation only:

- Run the builder and all builder-authored tests inside a real OS boundary and standalone clone.
- Permit only regular text files and a small linear change.
- Reject binaries, symlinks, gitlinks, attributes, hooks, executable-mode changes, merge commits, unexpected control files, and oversized bundles.
- Produce a full commit, tree manifest, diff artifact, and test evidence.
- Run tool-less, sandboxed reviewers if those are ready.
- Stop at a reviewed candidate. Do not merge automatically overnight.

Build order should be:

1. Execution boundary for builder, tests, and reviewers.
2. Immutable artifact contract and full base/tip/tree binding.
3. Canonical renderer and Git policy checks.
4. Real cross-family tester with Feature Acceptance Checklist evidence.
5. Reliable review roster and capacity reservation.
6. Builder integration.
7. Automatic merge last.

Task 4 overlaps the same completion path and should not merge first. Its in-flight branch automatically runs a newly invented tester and then asks for merge ([in-flight executor.py:164](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command-worktrees/gpt-merge-loop/harness/executor.py:164)). Keep task 4 at “ask the gatekeeper after valid tester evidence already exists” until this design is settled.

Hard STOP, rather than ADJUST:

- Running builder-authored code or tests as the owner.
- Using linked worktrees as the claimed isolation boundary.
- Letting tool-capable reviewers consume hostile diffs.
- Automatically merging a tree that was not itself the tested and reviewed integration result.

## Q9. Additional dangerous gaps

1. **The test runner is an arbitrary-code runner.** The in-flight tester executes builder-controlled Python tests as the owner ([in-flight tester.py:138](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command-worktrees/gpt-merge-loop/harness/tester.py:138)). A malicious `conftest.py` runs before any test and can steal secrets or modify shared Git state.

2. **Task 4 launders a unit-test log into app-driving evidence.** It records a plain test log as artifact kind `trace` ([in-flight tester.py:157](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command-worktrees/gpt-merge-loop/harness/tester.py:157)). That satisfies the database guard even though no browser drove the app. The schema says traces are evidence that the app was driven ([schema.sql:524](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/db/schema.sql:524)), and the owner requires checklist-driven PRESENT/MISSING/BROKEN/UNREACHABLE verification ([AGENTS.md:53](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/AGENTS.md:53)). This is a direct semantic bypass.

3. **Linked worktrees are not isolation.** The executor claims the builder “physically cannot scribble on main” ([executor.py:10](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/executor.py:10)), but `git worktree add` shares refs, objects, config, and hooks ([executor.py:114](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/executor.py:114)). A builder with shell access can move `refs/heads/main`, alter hooks/config, delete objects, or corrupt the repository.

4. **Worktree reuse is unverified.** An existing job directory is accepted without checking its resolved path, cleanliness, HEAD, or ownership ([executor.py:109](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/executor.py:109)). A stale directory or planted symlink can redirect the builder elsewhere.

5. **Plain-directory fallback is invalid for code builds.** If worktree creation fails, the executor silently continues with an empty directory ([executor.py:119](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/executor.py:119)). Diff-reviewed jobs must fail outright.

6. **Tests do not run from a sterile copy of the reviewed commit.** They run in the builder’s original working directory. Ignored or uncommitted files can make tests pass even though those files will not merge.

7. **Git hooks remain executable authority.** Both builder commits and gatekeeper merges invoke Git normally ([executor.py:75](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/executor.py:75), [gatekeeper.py:406](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/gatekeeper.py:406)). Shared or reconfigured hooks can execute arbitrary commands under the harness identity.

8. **The acceptance checklist is absent from the panel prompt.** The current prompt asks only whether the work is correct ([gauntlet.py:66](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/gauntlet.py:66)). It cannot enforce the owner-locked completeness and reachability gate.

9. **Secret exfiltration can happen through the panel.** If a same-user builder reads a secret and places it in a diff, the harness sends that diff to three external providers before anyone detects it. Secret scanning must precede external review.

10. **The proposed memory input is actively wrong.** The established lane memories are for the medical-records project and contain incompatible conventions. They must not be prepended. Use this repository’s instructions and a new lane memory created for this project.

## VERDICT: STOP

Required changes, ordered by risk removed:

1. Put builders, builder-authored tests, and CLI reviewers behind a real OS boundary with a dedicated home, standalone clone, restricted network, and no owner secrets or live-control access.
2. Disable automatic merging until the exact prospective merge tree against a fixed `main` tip has been tested and reviewed; any change to `main` creates a new integration version.
3. Replace linked worktrees as the isolation claim. Builders must never share refs, config, hooks, or object storage with the real repository.
4. Create an immutable per-job artifact contract and reviewed-bundle record containing full base/tip/tree IDs, mode, manifest, renderer identity, and digest; verdicts must cite it.
5. Make the tester run a sterile checkout of the exact candidate and verify the Feature Acceptance Checklist in the running app. A unit-test log must not qualify as browser/app evidence.
6. Harden Git input: reject merge commits, gitlinks, symlinks, binaries, attributes/filter changes, executable-mode changes, hooks, and unsupported control files for the first release.
7. Make reviewers tool-less and parse a schema-constrained out-of-band verdict. Do not search arbitrary stdout for lines beginning with `PASS`.
8. Make the Claude-build review roster operationally reliable: reserve review/test capacity before building and provide a reliable non-Claude fallback. Fix retry reuse before budget checks.
9. Replace truncation with preflight refusal and, later, complete chunk-coverage review.
10. Store full commit IDs; validate fresh clean workspaces; forbid plain-directory fallback; snapshot the actual provider, resolved model, runner, and pinned CLI version.
tokens used
173,861
The plan is not safe for unattended execution or automatic merging tonight. The diff hash is not the main problem. The hard failures are that unreviewed code and its tests run as the owner, linked worktrees share Git’s control plane with `main`, and the reviewers themselves inherit dangerous tools and credentials.

## Q1. Reviewed-diff binding

The premise is slightly wrong today: `head_version` is not the full commit SHA. The executor truncates it to 16 hexadecimal characters, and the gatekeeper accepts a prefix match ([executor.py:89](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/executor.py:89), [gatekeeper.py:350](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/gatekeeper.py:350)). That is probably collision-resistant enough accidentally, but it is not the claimed exact binding. Store the full object ID.

With a full commit ID, the SHA already binds the commit’s complete tree and parent graph. Hashing a diff does not strengthen that binding. Its real purpose is different: proving that the projection shown to reviewers faithfully represented that commit. The current system needs such a check because the version and reviewed text are independent inputs ([executor.py:212](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/executor.py:212), [executor.py:227](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/executor.py:227)).

Therefore:

- SHA comparison answers: “Is this still the same commit?”
- Bundle comparison answers: “Did reviewers see an adequate and authentic rendering of that commit?”
- A hash of an unstable, incomplete rendering merely authenticates the incompleteness.

Specific under-representation cases:

| Mechanism | What reviewers may miss |
|---|---|
| Binary files | Normal `git diff` says only that binary files differ. Arbitrary payload bytes ship unseen. `--binary` makes a transport patch, not a meaningful security review. |
| Submodules/gitlinks | The diff normally shows an old and new commit ID, not the referenced repository content. A builder can create mode `160000` without a pre-existing `.gitmodules`. Ban gitlinks initially. |
| Symlinks | Git shows mode `120000` and the target string, not what that target resolves to. A merged symlink can make later tests or tools read or overwrite files outside the repository. |
| Executable/mode changes | Git does show `old mode/new mode`, but this is easy for a prose reviewer to miss. A separate path/mode/blob manifest must make it explicit. |
| `.gitattributes` | `-diff`, custom diff drivers, `textconv`, encoding rules, and LFS filters can suppress, transform, or replace what reviewers see. An LFS pointer can be reviewed while external content later appears in the checkout. |
| Shared `.git/config` | This is worse than under-representation: it is an out-of-band mutation absent from every commit and diff. A linked-worktree builder can poison diff drivers, merge drivers, hooks, filters, and Git behavior before the gatekeeper runs. |
| Rename detection | `-M` can compress a delete/add into a rename plus partial hunks. It is tree-equivalent, but rendering and reviewer perception become heuristic and configuration-dependent. Pin `--no-renames` or one exact policy. |
| CRLF and checkout conversion | Tree-to-tree diff compares committed blobs, so `core.autocrlf` is less dangerous than claimed for the committed tree. But checkout bytes may differ through EOL, encoding, filter, or attribute conversion. Blob IDs must be authoritative; test the exact checkout policy used in production. |
| No common history | `merge-base` failure must be a hard refusal. Substituting the empty tree changes the review from “topic delta” into “entire repository” and still does not make unrelated-history merging safe. |
| Merge commits in the job | Endpoint diff omits intermediate commits and second-parent history. Content added and later removed is absent from the final tree diff but becomes reachable from `main` after merging, including accidentally committed secrets. |
| Branch merges `main` | The latest common ancestor can advance, and second-parent history is hidden. Conflict-resolution changes appear only as net tree changes; the history and integration decision do not. Forbid merge commits in the first version. |
| Two-dot versus three-dot | `git diff A..B` is simply endpoint tree comparison. `git diff main...tip` compares `merge-base(main,tip)` to `tip`. If `base` is already the exact merge base, `base..tip` and `base...tip` are equivalent. `main..tip` is not: it includes inverse changes from `main` that the topic does not contain. |

The largest omission is the prospective merge result. The gatekeeper performs a real three-way merge but never verifies the resulting tree against a reviewed tree ([gatekeeper.py:284](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/gatekeeper.py:284), [gatekeeper.py:305](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/gatekeeper.py:305)). Custom merge drivers or new changes on `main` can produce a tree the panel never saw.

## Q2. Merge-base drift

I disagree with the premise that every ordinary advance of `main` changes the merge base. If `main` advances normally and the fixed topic tip has not incorporated those commits, the merge base usually remains the original branch point.

That does not make the plan safe. It exposes a worse failure:

- The stored diff can compare equal.
- The merge base can remain unchanged.
- Yet merging into the newer `main` can conflict semantically or produce a different integrated result.

Record, immutably:

- Full base commit ID.
- Full tip commit ID and tip tree ID.
- Canonical bundle digest.
- Exact renderer version/options.
- Complete path/mode/blob manifest.
- Prospective merge-tree ID against the exact `main` tip being integrated.

At merge time, either `main` must still equal the recorded integration tip, or the system must generate a new prospective merge tree, run the tests against it, and review it as a new version. Recomputing against a dynamic merge base is the wrong invariant.

## Q3. Mode selection

The mode must be immutable job history, not inferred from the current seat provider.

A job needs an explicit contract such as `text-result` or `git-tree`, fixed at dispatch before the builder starts. Better still, use an immutable reviewed-bundle record containing mode, base, tip, tree, manifest digest, rendered digest, size, and renderer identity. Every verdict should cite that bundle, not merely the tip SHA.

This cannot safely be derived later from `seats.provider`:

- The executor selects the runner using the seat’s live provider ([executor.py:176](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/executor.py:176)).
- Server synchronization rewrites provider/model/family/tier from configuration ([dispatch.py:262](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/dispatch.py:262), [jobs.py:95](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/db/jobs.py:95)).
- The existing identity guard freezes seat/tier/family but not provider, resolved model, runner type, or review mode ([schema.sql:677](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/db/schema.sql:677)).
- `jobs.result` is not frozen; ordinary status updates can replace it even after review ([jobs.py:224](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/db/jobs.py:224)).

The mode and bundle must be unchangeable after insertion—and certainly after the first verdict. Snapshot the actual provider, resolved model, CLI version, and runner contract too.

## Q4. Family arithmetic

A Claude-built job has exactly two diversity-contributing families: GPT and Grok. The Claude reviewer is still required as a seat, but is excluded from the diversity count ([schema.sql:895](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/db/schema.sql:895)). The configured roster and floor are three seats and two non-builder families ([seats.toml:357](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/config/seats.toml:357)).

Operational behavior:

- Missing Grok key before dispatch: Grok is excluded by readiness checking ([gauntlet.py:211](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/gauntlet.py:211)). After excluding the Claude builder family, only GPT remains, so dispatch is refused ([dispatch.py:240](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/dispatch.py:240)).
- Grok already capped at dispatch: the roster check does not inspect its role cap. The Claude build can run, then Grok skips during review ([gauntlet.py:368](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/gauntlet.py:368)). The job parks with only one qualifying family ([gauntlet.py:536](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/gauntlet.py:536)).
- Grok becomes capped or loses its key after dispatch: same parked result.
- Even if the family floor somehow passed, the full-panel guard requires every stamped reviewer seat to pass ([schema.sql:865](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/db/schema.sql:865)).

There is also a retry bug: `_review_one` checks current budget before looking for an already-recorded verdict. A prior Grok pass is ignored on a retry if Grok is now capped ([gauntlet.py:368](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/gauntlet.py:368), [gauntlet.py:377](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/gauntlet.py:377)).

A Claude-family tester would be rejected for a Claude build ([schema.sql:847](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/db/schema.sql:847)), leaving no acceptable tester and causing shipping to fail ([schema.sql:1000](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/db/schema.sql:1000)).

The in-flight task-4 implementation does choose the first different-family reviewer, so it selects GPT for a Claude build ([in-flight tester.py:109](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command-worktrees/gpt-merge-loop/harness/tester.py:109)). That arithmetic is compatible. Its testing semantics are not; see Q9.

Conclusion: Claude builds work only while all three reviewers are healthy and Grok has both credentials and reserved capacity. That is zero operational slack and is not suitable for an unattended night. A reliable roster or provider-level fallback must land first.

## Q5. Prompt injection into the panel

Passing the prompt as a `subprocess` argument list is not shell injection. Builder text cannot create new command-line arguments because no shell splitting occurs.

The real attacks are:

1. Semantic prompt injection: malicious comments or strings in the diff tell the reviewer to ignore the review and answer `PASS`.
2. Parser confusion: any output line beginning with `PASS`, including unintended prefixes, counts ([gauntlet.py:79](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/gauntlet.py:79)).
3. CLI echo/log confusion: arbitrary stdout is searched backwards rather than parsing one trusted result field.
4. Reviewer tool abuse: Claude and Codex reviewers currently inherit their working directory, environment, and default tools ([gauntlet.py:113](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/gauntlet.py:113), [gauntlet.py:136](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/gauntlet.py:136)). An injected diff can ask a reviewer to modify `main`, read secrets, or send them elsewhere.

Minimal structural fix:

- Reviewers run with no file-edit, shell, web, MCP, or inherited settings; preferably in a separate sandbox.
- Instructions occupy the highest available prompt channel; the bundle is clearly untrusted data.
- Each runner returns one schema-constrained object containing exact enum `pass|fail`, reason, bundle digest, reviewer identity, and invocation ID.
- Parse only the structured final-result field, never arbitrary stdout.
- Require exact equality, not `startswith`.

A nonce helps reject stale or echoed responses. It does not defeat prompt injection because the model can see the nonce. JSON output fixes parsing, not a reviewer persuaded to return a valid but dishonest `pass`.

